tara-kb:S-112625-100 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-100" ;
    dc:title "Superficial acupuncture in the relief of chronic low back pain A placebo controlled randomised trial" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol """Trigger points identified by palpation using methods described by Travell and Macdonald.
Commonly occurring painful regions: erector spinae, multifidus, iliocostalis, quadratus lumborum, iliopsoas, obliquus externus and internus abdominis, rectus abdominis, and muscles of the hip and lower limb.
Needles inserted approximately 4 mm deep into skin and subcutaneous layers over trigger points, avoiding muscle penetration.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Randomized Controlled Trial (RCT)" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails "The control group consisted of 9 patients who received placebo treatment, involving the attachment of inert surface electrodes to mimic electrical stimulation without actual delivery. This was used to compare the efficacy of superficial acupuncture against a sham intervention." ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupoints "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasMappedWesternCondition HP:0003419 ;
    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails "4 mm" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-100 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-100 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails "Total sample size: 17; Acupuncture group: 8; Placebo group: 9" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails """Sham procedure: Attachment of inert surface electrodes to the skin over tender muscle regions.
Needle type: None (electrodes used instead).
Quantity and size: Standard electroencephalographic electrodes (1 cm in diameter).
Location: Over tender muscle regions.
Penetrating or non penetrating: Non penetrating.
Other details: Electrodes attached to a dummy apparatus with a "whirring" sound.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails """Initial stimulation: Noxious stimulus from superficial needle insertion.
If ineffective: Duration increased up to 20 minutes.
If still ineffective: Electroacupuncture with impulses of 700 ps duration at 2 Hz frequency, amplitude increased every 2 minutes to maintain stimulation just above pain threshold.
Sensation sought: Vasodilatation and feeling of warmth.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper does not provide TCM terminology for the condition)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Chronic low back pain" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "Yes, the acupuncture intervention was effective compared to the control/sham group based on the primary outcomes, with statistical significance (p<0.01)." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measures:
Pain relief (%)
PainScore reduction (%)
ActivityPainScore reduction (%)
PhysicalSigns reduction (%)
Severity andPain area reduction (%)
Secondary outcome measures:
Mood assessment (activity, appetite,Sleep, anxiety)
Clinical assessments (gait impairment,Spinal mobility impairment, etc.)""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """Duration of first treatment: 5 minutes
Maximum duration: 20 minutes""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "Once a week; maximum of 10 treatments; initial duration of 5 minutes, increasing up to 20 minutes if necessary." .

