    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails """Total participants analyzed: 11
Participants within groups: Not specified (crossover design with 22 courses)""" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails "Not applicable (the study did not use sham acupuncture)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails "Not specified (the paper does not provide details on how the needles were stimulated)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper does not explicitly mention a TCM disease or condition)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting in pediatric oncology patients with Ewing's sarcoma, rhabdomyosarcoma, and osteosarcoma" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings """Direct quote: "The benefits of acupuncture in adolescents with respect to the reduction of additional antiemetic medication were observed."
Effectiveness: Yes (reduction in additional antiemetic medication)""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measure: Reduction of the need for antiemetic medication by use of acupuncture.
Secondary outcome measures: Average number of episodes of vomiting per day, subjective nausea score concerning sensations of nausea, vomiting, and appetite.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """Duration of each acupuncture session: about 20 minutes
How long the needles were left in place during a session: about 20 minutes""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "Acupuncture was administered on the first day of chemotherapy and then on subsequent days as needed by the patient. Each session lasted approximately 20 minutes." .

tara-kb:S-112625-1028 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-1028" ;
    dc:title "Electroacupuncture Prophylaxis of Postoperative Nausea and Vomiting following Pediatric Tonsillectomy with or without Adenoidectomy" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol """P6: Located 2 Chinese inches proximal to the distal skin crease of the wrist, in the anterior antebrachial region on the ulnar side of the tendon of the flexor carpi radialis.
Neutral point: Midway up the forearm, on the same meridian but on a different dermatome (T1).
P2: Located in the anterior brachial region, 2 cuns distal to the end of the anterior axillary fold between the two heads of the biceps brachii muscle.
Neutral point: High on the arm, on the ventral surface in the same acupuncture meridian as P2.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Randomized Controlled Trial (RCT)" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails """Acupuncture group: Received P6 electroacupuncture with electrical stimulation.
Sham acupuncture group: Received sham needles at points not associated with antiemesis.
Control group: No needles were inserted; insulated wires were attached to maintain blinding.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "United States" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupoints "P 6, P 2" ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupointsMapped TARA:4311153,
        TARA:4819578 ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupointsMappedCurie "{\"TARA:4311153\": \"PC 6\", \"TARA:4819578\": \"PC 2\"}"^^rdf:JSON ;
    tara-kb:hasMappedWesternCondition HP:0002017 ;
    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails "1 cm" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-1028 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-1028 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails """Total sample size: 120
Participants within groups: 40 per group (acupuncture, sham acupuncture, control)""" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails """Needle type: Real needles
Quantity: Two needles
Size: Not specified
Location: P2 and a neutral point (not associated with antiemesis)
Penetrating: Yes
Other details: Needles were inserted high on the arm, on the ventral surface, in the same acupuncture meridian but outside the dermatome of P6.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails "The stimulation used was low-frequency electrical stimulation (electroacupuncture) at 4 Hz for 20 minutes. No other type of sensation or stimulation method was mentioned." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper does not explicitly mention the condition in TCM terminology)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Postoperative nausea and vomiting (PONV)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings """The P6 acupuncture significantly reduced the incidence of PONV compared to the control group (P < 0.001).
The occurrence of nausea was significantly less in the P6 acupuncture group compared to both the sham and control groups (P < 0.01).
The incidence of vomiting was not significantly different between the P6 acupuncture group and the control group.
The study concludes that P6 acupuncture is effective in reducing nausea but not vomiting, and its efficacy is comparable to commonly used pharmacotherapies.
Yes, the acupuncture intervention was effective compared to the control/sham group(s) based on the primary outcome(s).""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measures: Incidence of nausea, incidence of vomiting, need for rescue therapy in the first 24 postoperative hours.
Secondary outcome measure: Time to vomiting.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration "Duration of each acupuncture session: 20 minutes; Duration needles were left in place: Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "Not specified (the paper describes a single session of acupuncture treatment lasting 20 minutes, but does not provide information on the frequency of treatment beyond this session)" .

