    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails "Manual manipulation was used to stimulate the needles, with the aim of strengthening the needling sensation. Needles were manipulated once every 10 minutes and retained for 30 minutes." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Knee osteoarthritis: Ben-root deficiency (liver, kidney, yang-qi), Biao-branch excess (cold, blood stasis, dampness)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Knee osteoarthritis" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings """Results: The total effective rates were 91.4% for abdominal acupuncture, 88.6% for conventional acupuncture, and 97.1% for the combined group. There was a remarkable difference in effect among the groups (P<0.05), and the difference in Lysholm knee scoring scale scores was very significant (P<0.01).
Conclusion: Abdominal acupuncture has a reliable effect for knee osteoarthritis, and combining it with conventional acupuncture enhances the therapeutic effect.
Effectiveness: Yes""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measure: Lysholm knee scoring scale
Secondary outcome measures: Total effective rate, scores before and after treatment as presented in the tables""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration "30 minutes (duration of each session); 30 minutes (needles left in place during a session)" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "Treatment was given once a day, 6 times a week, for 4 weeks. Each session lasted 30 minutes with needle manipulation every 10 minutes." .

