tara-kb:S-112625-116 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-116" ;
    dc:title "Acupuncture and Dry-Needling for Low Back Pain: An Updated Systematic Review Within the Framework of the Cochrane Collaboration" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol """Acupuncture: Classic meridian points, extra points, ah-shi points (painful points)
Dry-needling: Myofascial trigger points identified by palpation
Points chosen based on traditional acupuncture theory to restore balance between Yin and Yang forces
Modern acupuncturists use both traditional meridian points and nonmeridian points
Specific points not comprehensively listed in the paper""" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Randomized Controlled Trial (RCT)" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails """No treatment
Sham acupuncture
Sham transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation (TENS)
Chinese herbal medicine
Education
Exercise
Massage
Moxibustion
Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs)
Physiotherapy
Spinal manipulation
TENS
Trigger point injections
Usual treatment by a general practitioner""" ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "Not specified (the paper is a systematic review including studies from multiple countries and does not specify a single country where the review was conducted)" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasMappedWesternCondition HP:0003419,
        MONDO:0006862 ;
    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails "Not specified (the paper does not provide a uniform or specific depth for needle insertion across all studies)" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-116 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-116 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails """Total participants: 2861
Participants with chronic low back pain: 1718
Specific comparisons:
Acupuncture vs. no treatment: 90 people
Acupuncture vs. sham therapy: 314 people
Addition of acupuncture to other interventions: 289 people (immediately after sessions), 182 people (short-term follow-up)""" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails """For acute low Back Pain: Sham needling of the SI3 acupoint.
For Chronic Low Back Pain: Specific details on sham procedure not provided in the text.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails """Manual stimulation: Needles were manually manipulated to achieve Teh Chi sensation.
Electrical stimulation (electroacupuncture): An electrical stimulator was connected to the acupuncture needle.
Moxibustion: The moxa herb was burned at the end of the needle.
Not all studies specified the type of stimulation used.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper uses Western medical terminology for conditions)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Nonspecific low back pain and myofascial pain syndrome in the low back region" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "Yes, acupuncture is effective compared to the control/sham group for chronic low back pain in terms of pain relief and functional improvement in the short term. However, it is not more effective than other conventional or alternative treatments. For acute low back pain, the evidence is inconclusive." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcomes: Pain intensity (e.g., visual analogue scale [VAS]), back specific functional status (e.g., Roland Disability Scale, Oswestry Scale)
Secondary outcomes: Physiologic outcomes of physical examination (e.g., range of motion, spinal flexibility), generic health status (e.g., Short Form 36, Nottingham Health Profile), other symptoms (e.g., medication use, side effects)""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration "Not specified (the paper does not provide a uniform duration for all studies)" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "Not specified (the paper does not provide a uniform frequency of treatment across all studies, but specific details can be found in the tables)" .

