tara-kb:S-112625-1171 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-1171" ;
    dc:title "Acupuncture in the Treatment of Hypertension: A Literature Review" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Systematic Review" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails """SHARP study: Invasive sham acupuncture with needles inserted in non TCM meridian points without stimulation.
Yin et al. study: Non invasive sham group.
Zhang et al. study: Sham group receiving treatment with no power output from the Hans electrical stimulator.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasMappedWesternCondition HP:0000822 ;
    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-1171 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-1171 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails "Not specified (the paper does not provide a total sample size for all participants across the included RCTs)" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails """Sham procedure involved inserting needles in non TCM meridian points.
Needles were inserted without stimulation.
Sham procedure was non penetrating or minimal penetration.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails "Electrical stimulation was used in the trials. Needles were stimulated with electrical stimulation in the individualized group and in the trial by Zhang et al. No other forms of stimulation were mentioned. No specific sensation was sought during the stimulation." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Flare up of liver fire Liver yang rising with kidney yin deficiency Obstruction of phlegm and dampness Yin and yang deficiency Qi and blood deficiency leading to liver yang rising" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Hypertension" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings """The results showed that mean 24 hour ambulatory systolic and diastolic blood pressures decreased significantly after treatment in the TCM group.
The result of a smaller RCT supported that acupuncture lowered systolic blood pressure.
Compared with the sham group receiving treatment with no power output from the Hans electrical stimulator, the real electrical acupuncture group was treated twice a week for five weeks and significantly reduced systolic blood pressure after ten treatments.
In the end, the real acupuncture group demonstrated a significant decrease in systolic and diastolic blood pressures at eight weeks.
Final results indicated the mean values decreased from baseline to the tenth week, but it did not differ significantly between participants randomly assigned to individualized and standardized groups versus sham acupuncture.
Thus, this literature review has demonstrated the evidence of acupunctures modulating function on blood pressure.
Among the select acupuncture points, Quchi LI 11 might be effective to induce blood pressure reduction.
From this perspective, the clinical utility of acupuncture to treat hypertension and other related cardiovascular diseases deserves additional research.
The effectiveness of acupuncture compared to the control/sham group: Maybe/Inconclusive""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measures: Changes inSystolic and diastolic bloodPressures
Secondary outcome measures: NotSpecified""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """Duration of each acupuncture session: 30 minutes
Frequency: Twice a week for 5 weeks; 22 sessions in 6 weeks
Duration retained: Not specified""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency """Twice a week for five weeks (10 treatments)
Three to four days interval for eight weeks
Up to 12 sessions in six to eight weeks
17 sessions with 3 4 day intervals in 8 weeks
22 sessions, 30 minutes each, in 6 weeks
30 minutes per session, twice a week for 5 weeks""" .

