    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "Yes, electro-acupuncture was found to be effective compared to the control group (amitriptyline), with equal curative effects and higher efficiency index scores. Statistical significance was noted within groups (P<0.01) but not between groups (P>0.05)." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcomes: Number andSeverity of VMS, MenopauseSpecific Quality of Life Questionnaire
Secondary outcomes: Beck Depression Inventory,SpielbergState Trait Anxiety Instrument,Pittsburgh QualitySleep Index, 24 hour urine cortisol and metabolites, adrenocorticotropic hormoneStimulation testing""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration "Not specified (the paper does not provide information on the duration of each acupuncture session or how long the needles were left in place)" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "Not specified (the paper does not provide details on the frequency of acupuncture treatments)" .

tara-kb:S-112625-1202 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-1202" ;
    dc:title "Acupuncture for postmenopausal hot flashes" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol """Active treatment: 5-7 points, 3-6 addressing primary TCM pattern, 1-2 addressing secondary patterns.
Placebo treatment: 5-7 sham points off any acupuncture channels.
Points selected based on TCM diagnosis using a standardized diagnostic algorithm and treatment manual.
Specific points listed in tables at the end of the paper.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Randomized Controlled Trial (RCT) Pilot Study" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails """No acupuncture: acute treatment only or routine care
Sham acupuncture: varied techniques including superficial needling of non-acupuncture points or placebo needles
Prophylactic drug treatment: metoprolol, flunarizine, or individualized treatment
Other treatments: relaxation programs, combination of massage and relaxation""" ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "United States" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupoints "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasMappedWesternCondition HP:0031217 ;
    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails "Not specified (the paper does not provide specific information on the depth of needle insertion for the active treatment group)" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-1202 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-1202 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails """Total sample size: 4419
Vickers 2004: 401
Jena 2008: 1715
Jena 2008 distribution: Immediate acupuncture = 1613, Routine care before acupuncture = 1569""" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails """In three trials, existing acupuncture points considered inadequate for migraine treatment were needled superficially.
In five trials, superficial needling of non-acupuncture points at variable distances from true points was used.
In two trials, close non-acupuncture points were needled without specified depth.
In two trials, 'placebo' needles (telescope needles with blunt tips not penetrating the skin) were used.
In one trial, placebo needles were placed at correct, individualized points after a full Chinese diagnosis process.
In another trial, placebo needles were placed at standardized points without the 'Chinese ritual'.
Two trials used other sham interventions without skin penetration.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Kidney yin vacuity, vacuity heat; Ascendant hyperactive Lv yang; Phlegm Fire" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Postmenopausal hot flashes" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings """The paper concludes that acupuncture is effective as an adjunct to routine care and at least as effective as prophylactic drug treatment.
However, true acupuncture interventions do not seem to be superior to sham interventions.
The primary outcome measure of responder rate ratios did not show significant differences between acupuncture and sham.
Therefore, the effectiveness of acupuncture compared to sham is inconclusive based on the primary outcome.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measures: Global index of the severity and frequency of hot flashes per 24 hours (derived from participants' daily logs)
Secondary outcome measure: Assessment of improvement in quality of life (measured by the menopausal specific quality of life questionnaire, MSQL)""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """General duration: Needles left in place between 20 and 30 minutes
Exceptions:
Dowson 1985: Needles inserted for 10 minutes
Hesse 1994: Brief needling for a few seconds""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "Not specified (the paper provides details on the number and duration of sessions but not on the specific frequency of treatments per week or month)" .

