tara-kb:S-112625-1213 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-1213" ;
    dc:title "Clinical Comparison of Motor Conduction of Facial Nerve Branches: A Randomized Controlled Trial of Using Acupuncture to Treat Bell's Palsy" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol "Not specified (the paper does not provide information on specific acupuncture points or needle locations)" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Randomized Controlled Trial (RCT)" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails "The control group consisted of patients who received treatment with Western medicine alone, without acupuncture or moxibustion. The number of patients in the control group was 69 and 83 cases. The control group was used as a comparison to evaluate the effects of acupuncture and moxibustion on motor conduction of facial nerve branches." ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "China" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupoints "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasMappedWesternCondition MONDO:0005665 ;
    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails "Not specified (the paper does not provide information on needle insertion depth)" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-1213 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-1213 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails """Total participants: 480 initially entered, but only 205 and 246 completed tests for buccal and temporal branches, respectively.
Control group: 69 and 83 cases (pre/post treatment: 64 and 79 cases).
Treatment group 1: 70 and 83 cases (pre/post treatment: 63 and 74 cases).
Treatment group 2: 66 and 80 cases (pre/post treatment: 62 and 77 cases).""" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails "Not applicable (no sham acupuncture group was used in the study)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails "Not specified (the paper does not provide details on how acupuncture needles were stimulated or if any specific sensations were sought)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper does not provide a TCM term for the condition)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Bell's Palsy" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings """Results: Delayed latency of the buccal branch improved post treatment (P<0.05), but amplitude did not improve (P>0.05). Amplitude of the temporal branch improved significantly post treatment (P<0.01), but latency did not improve (P>0.05).
Conclusion: Recovery of the temporal branch is better than that of the buccal branch.
Effectiveness of Acupuncture: Not specified (the paper does not explicitly compare acupuncture's effectiveness against a control group).""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measures:
Amplitude in actionPotentials of motor conduction of the facial nerve
Latency in actionPotential of the buccal and temporal branches of the facial nerve
Secondary outcome measures:
Clinical manifestation and recovery rates (implied)""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration "Not specified (the paper does not provide details on the duration of each acupuncture session or the retention time of the needles)" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "Not specified (the paper does not provide information on the frequency of acupuncture treatments)" .

tara-kb:S-112625-1214 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-1214" ;
    dc:title "A prospective randomised controlled study on efficacies of acupuncture and steroid in treatment of idiopathic peripheral facial paralysis" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol "Not specified (the paper does not provide specific acupoints or needle locations used in the acupuncture treatment)" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Randomized Controlled Trial (RCT)" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails "The control group in this study is the superficial needle insertion (SNI) group, which was used as a control method because it elicits weaker peripheral stimulation compared to electroacupuncture (EA). However, it is noted that SNI cannot be considered a plain placebo method due to its peripheral sensory stimulation." ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "Hong Kong" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupoints "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasMappedWesternCondition MONDO:0001835 ;
    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-1214 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-1214 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails """Total participants analyzed: 21
Participants within groups: Group 1 = 11, Group 2 = 10""" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails "Not applicable (the paper does not use a sham acupuncture group; instead, it uses superficial needle insertion as a control method)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails """Electroacupuncture (EA) group: Electrical stimulation at 2 Hz; Needles rotated to elicit the sensation of Teh Chi.
Superficial needle insertion (SNI) group: No additional stimulation beyond subcutaneous insertion; No sensation sought.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper describes the TCM concept but does not provide a specific TCM term or diagnosis for the condition)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Idiopathic peripheral facial paralysis (Bell's palsy)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "Yes, the acupuncture intervention was effective compared to the control/sham group(s) based on the primary outcome(s)." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measures:
Degree of recovery: Difference between initial and final grades on the House-Brackmann facial nerve grading system.
Speed of recovery: Time required to achieve a stable grade from onset of treatment.
Secondary outcome measures: Not explicitly mentioned, but tables provide data on the number of patients who improved at different time points.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """Duration of each acupuncture session: 30 minutes
Duration needles were left in place: 30 minutes""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "Twice a week for 2 weeks, then once a week for 6 weeks, with each session lasting 30 minutes. Total duration of treatment: 8 weeks." .

