Improved: Deviation of the gazePosition of the affected eye was improved, with reduction inSpace between the double ocular images for aSingle object, and a relief of limited movement of the affected eyeball.
Failed: No improvement inStrabismus and the accompanyingSymptoms.
Secondary outcome measures: NotSpecified""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration "30 minutes (duration of each acupuncture session), 30 minutes (needles retained during a session)" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "The acupuncture treatment was administered once daily, with each session lasting 30 minutes and involving manipulation every 15 minutes. The treatment was structured into 10 day courses, with effects evaluated after each course, and patients typically underwent three courses with a 5 day interval between them." .

tara-kb:S-112625-1233 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-1233" ;
    dc:title "Randomized Controlled Trial of Patching vs Acupuncture for Anisometropic Amblyopia in Children Aged 7 to 12 Years" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol """GV20 (baihui): On top of the head, 7 cun above the occipital hairline and 5 cun behind the frontal hairline; perpendicular or oblique insertion; depth 1-2 mm
EX-HN5 (taiyang): At the temporal fossa and 1 cun behind the midpoint of a line from the lateral end of the eyebrow to the external canthus; perpendicular or oblique insertion; depth 6-10 mm
BL2 (cuanzhu): In the supraorbital notch and at the median end of the eyebrow; perpendicular or oblique insertion; depth 2-6 mm
LI4 (hegu): At the dorsum of the hand and between the first and second metacarpals, midway down the second metacarpal; perpendicular insertion; depth 10-15 mm
BL59 (fuyang): 3 cun above the site between the Achilles tendon and lateral malleolus; oblique insertion; depth 10-20 mm""" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Randomized Controlled Trial (RCT)" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails "The control group in this study is the patching group, which received optical correction plus 2 hours of daily patching and 1 hour of near-vision activity. This is considered the mainstream therapy for anisometropic amblyopia in older children and serves as the comparison group for evaluating the effectiveness of acupuncture." ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "China" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupoints "GV 20, EX-HN 5, BL 2, LI 4, BL 59" ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupointsMapped TARA:0859695,
        TARA:3386254,
        TARA:4995021,
        TARA:8104948,
        TARA:9534465 ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupointsMappedCurie "{\"TARA:3386254\": \"GV 20\", \"TARA:9534465\": \"Taiyang\", \"TARA:8104948\": \"BL 2\", \"TARA:0859695\": \"LI 4\", \"TARA:4995021\": \"BL 59\"}"^^rdf:JSON ;
    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-1233 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-1233 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails """Total sample size: 88
Patching group: 42
Acupuncture group: 41""" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails "Not applicable (no sham acupuncture group was used in the study)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails "Manual manipulation by rotation to achieve de Qi sensation." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper does not provide a TCM term for anisometropic amblyopia)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Anisometropic Amblyopia" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings """"The mean BSCVA of the amblyopic eye at 15 weeks improved from baseline by 1.83 and 2.27 lines in the patching and acupuncture groups, respectively."
"After baseline adjustment, the mean difference of B3>CVA between the 2 groups was 0.049 logMAR (95% confi-dence interval, 0.005-0.092; P=.03), meeting the definition of equivalence (difference within 1 line)."
"The BSCVA had improved by 2 lines or more in 28 (66.7%) and 31 (75.6%) eyes in the patching and acupuncture groups, respectively."
"Amblyopia was resolved in 7 (16.7%) and 17 (41.5%) eyes in the patching and acupuncture groups, respectively."
"Acupuncture produced equivalent treatment effect for anisometropic amblyopia, compared with patching, and was statistically superior."
"Conclusion: Acupuncture could potentially become an alternative treatment to occlusion therapy for amblyopia."
"The use of occlusion, mainly by patching simultaneously with optical correction, remains the mainstream therapy for anisometropic amblyopia in older children."
"The treatment effect of acupuncture is equivalent to that of patching for anisometropic amblyopia in older children."
"The resolution rate was significantly higher in the acupuncture group (41.5%) than in the patching group (16.7%) (P=.01)."
"The treatment effect of acupuncture appears promising, the mechanism underlying its success as a treatment for amblyopia remains unclear."
Yes""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measure: Best spectacle-corrected visual acuity (BSCVA) in the amblyopic eye at week 15.
Secondary outcome measures: BSCVA at other follow-up visits, visual acuity in the sound eye, responder rates, resolution rates.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """Duration of each acupuncture session: 15 minutes
Frequency of sessions: 5 sessions per week
Duration retained: Not specified (continued for the duration of the study)""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "5 treatment sessions per week, performed after school during weekdays, with a total of 125 sessions for most participants and at least 80 sessions for some." .

