Participants within groups: 9 patients per group (acupuncture first and placebo first)""" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails """Needle type: Real needles (length 25.4 mm, diameter 0.32 mm)
Quantity: Six needles
Location: Areas with no acupuncture points
Penetration: Superficial insertion to a depth of 2-4 mm
Other details: Designed to avoid the needling sensation""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails """Traditional Chinese Acupuncture: Needles were inserted into specific points, and a needling sensation was sought.
Placebo Acupuncture: Needles were inserted superficially without causing a needling sensation.
No additional stimulation methods (e.g., manual manipulation, electrical stimulation, thermal stimulation, laser) were mentioned.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper does not mention Traditional Chinese Medicine terminology or conditions)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Plantar Fasciitis (PF)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "Yes, traditional Chinese acupuncture was found to be significantly more effective than placebo acupuncture in reducing pain, with a 31% reduction in pain intensity. Statistical significance was indicated by p-values: Friedman two-way analysis of variance by ranks showed p = 0.025, and Wilcoxon's test for pair differences showed p < 0.05." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcomes:Pain description and mobility function at 1 and 2 months, measured with thePain and DisabilityScale forPF.
Secondary outcomes: MorningPain on a 100Point VAS at 1 monthPosttreatment, VAS for activityPain, overallPain rating, andPressurePain threshold using algometry.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """Duration of each acupuncture session: Not specified
Duration needles were left in place: 15 minutes""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "Twice weekly for six treatments over a three-week period" .

