tara-kb:S-112625-1255 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-1255" ;
    dc:title "Acupuncture for GI endoscopy: a systematic review" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol """Fanti et al. (2003): EA at L14, ST36, SP6, SP9 bilaterally, and Shenmen bilaterally.
Melchart et al. (2002): MA at ST36 bilaterally and CV6, CV12, CV22, and ear points stomach, spasmolysis, tai-jang unilaterally.
Stellon and Palmer (1999): MA at L14, PC6, ST9 bilaterally, and CV23, CV24.
Wang et al. (1997): EA + MA at ST36, ST37 unilaterally, and Shenmen bilaterally.
Wang et al. (1992): MA at ST36, ST37 unilaterally, and Shenmen bilaterally.
Cahn et al. (1978): EA at ST36, SP5, PC6 bilaterally, and CV12, CV17, CV23, CV24.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Randomized Controlled Trial (RCT)" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails """Sham acupuncture
Conventional premedication
No conventional treatment (no sedation)""" ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-1255 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-1255 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails "Total sample size: 512 patients; Specific group sizes are detailed in the tables at the end of the paper." ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails "The sham procedure involved needling non-acupuncture points. Specific details from the tables include: Fanti et al. (2003) used sham EA with procedures and stimulation analogous to EA but on inactive points. The paper does not specify needle type, quantity, size, or whether the sham was penetrating or non-penetrating." ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails """Electroacupuncture was used in two trials.
Manual acupuncture was used in three trials.
A combination of electroacupuncture and manual acupuncture was used in one trial.
Needles were stimulated to elicit a characteristic sensation called De-Qi.
Specific stimulation methods included electrical stimulation (electroacupuncture) and manual manipulation.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Not specified (the paper focuses on symptoms during GI endoscopy rather than a specific Western medical condition or disease)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "Yes (acupuncture was found to be effective compared to sham acupuncture and similar in effectiveness to conventional premedication)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measures:
Patient's pain intensity (visual analogue scale, Likert-type scale, yes/no answers)
Dosage of sedative medication given
Beta-endorphin level
Secondary outcome measures:
Patient and physician assessments of the procedure (verbal rating scale)
Number of attempts at intubation
Ease of intubation
Symptoms (e.g., nausea, dizziness)
Stress-related hormone levels
Blood pressure and heart rate
Willingness to accept the same method for another endoscopy""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """Fanti et al. (2003): EA for 20 minutes before and throughout the procedure
Melchart et al. (2002): MA prior to and throughout the procedure
Wang et al. (1997): EA + MA for 10 minutes before and throughout the procedure
Wang et al. (1992): MA for 15 minutes before and throughout the procedure
Cahn et al. (1978): EA for 10 minutes before and throughout the procedure
Stellon and Palmer (1999): MA for 5 minutes before and throughout the procedure""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "Not specified (the paper does not provide information on the frequency of treatment sessions beyond the timing and duration during endoscopic procedures)" .

