    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails """Sham acupuncture: Superficial needling at non-acupuncture points, 2-3 cm away from traditional points and not on a meridian, with an insertion depth of 1-2 mm.
Sham moxibustion: Holding the burning moxa pole 8 inches above and 2-3 cm away from traditional location for 2 minutes, without generating a heat sensation.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails """Needles were stimulated using manual manipulation: reinforcing/tonification method involved rotating the needle nine times in a clockwise direction, and the reducing method involved rotating six times in a counterclockwise direction.
Moxibustion was used as a form of thermal stimulation.
Sham acupuncture was minimally stimulating with no manipulation.
Placebo moxibustion did not generate heat.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper does not provide a specific TCM term for IBS)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Irritable Bowel Syndrome (IBS)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "Yes, the acupuncture intervention was effective compared to the control/sham group based on the primary outcomes of abdominal pain/discomfort and intestinal gas, bloating, and stool consistency, with statistically significant differences (p = .01)." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measures: Abdominal pain/discomfort, Composite IBS score (internal gas, bloating, and stool consistency)
Secondary outcome measure: Clinical Global Impression Scale (CGIS)""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration "Not specified (the paper does not provide specific details on the duration of each acupuncture session or how long the needles were left in place)" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "Twice-weekly for 4 weeks, totaling 8 sessions. Duration of each session not specified." .

