tara-kb:S-112625-1346 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-1346" ;
    dc:title "Effectiveness of Acupuncture for Palliative Care in Cancer Patients: A Systematic Review" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Randomized Controlled Trial (RCT)" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails """Western practice (e.g., Western medication and cognitive behavioral therapy)
Placebo treatment (e.g., sham acupuncture)
No treatment
Basic treatment (e.g., fasting, gastrointestinal pressure-relief, nutrition, and anti-inflammation)
Usual care
Sham acupuncture""" ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "China and other countries (not specified)" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasMappedWesternCondition MONDO:0004992 ;
    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-1346 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-1346 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails "Not specified (the paper does not provide a total sample size for all participants across the 33 RCTs)" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails """Gary D (2007): Sham acupuncture for 20 minutes, twice weekly for 4 weeks, with a crossover to true acupuncture starting at week 7.
Jill H (2009): Sham acupuncture for 30 minutes, twice weekly for the first 5 weeks, and then once a week for the following 5 weeks.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails "The stimulation in Dai-Han Zhou involves the use of thin, solid, metallic needles that are manipulated either manually or by electrical stimulation (electroacupuncture). Other methods such as thermal (moxibustion) or laser are not mentioned. The specific sensations sought during stimulation are not specified." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper does not explicitly mention TCM terminology for the conditions studied)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Cancer" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "Yes" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure "Primary outcomes: chemotherapy or radiotherapy-induced side effects, cancer pain, post-operative urinary retention, quality of life, vasomotor syndrome, post-operative gastrointestinal dysfunction, prevention of prolonged postoperative ileus, joint symptoms, immunomodulation. Secondary outcomes: volume of residual urine, hot flash frequency, time to first flatus and first bowel movement, WOMAC, M-SACRAH, BPI-SF, FACT-G." ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """Duration of each acupuncture session: Post-operative urinary retention: Not specified
Quality of life: Not specified
Vasomotor syndrome: 30 min, twice weekly for the first 5 weeks, thereafter once a week for the following 5 weeks
Other studies: Varies, see tables for specific details
Duration needles were left in place: Not specified""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency """Twice daily until gas passed from the anus (post-operative urinary retention)
Twice weekly for 4 weeks (vasomotor syndrome)
Twice daily for 4 consecutive postoperative days or until the first bowel movement (gastrointestinal function and prevention of prolonged postoperative ileus)
Twice weekly for 6 weeks (aromatase inhibitor-associated joint symptoms)""" .

