Control group: 46
Acupuncture group: 40
Sham-acupuncture group: 45""" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails """Sham procedure: Minimal acupuncture
Needle type: Real needles
Quantity: 20 sessions
Size: Not specified
Location: Non-acupuncture points, outside meridians
Penetration: Superficial insertion
Stimulation: No stimulation (no 'de qi')""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails """Traditional acupuncture: Needles were manually stimulated to elicit the "de qi" sensation of heaviness and numbness, lasting 5-20 seconds.
Sham-acupuncture: Needles were not stimulated.
Ear needles: Not stimulated.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper does not explicitly mention a TCM term for the condition being studied)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Chronic low-back pain (LBP)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings """Acupuncture was effective compared to the control group (physiotherapy) at the end of treatment for pain intensity, pain disability, and psychological distress.
Acupuncture was not effective compared to the sham-acupuncture group for pain intensity and disability, but was effective for psychological distress.
At 9-month follow-up, acupuncture was not significantly different from sham-acupuncture.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measures: Pain intensity (VAS-P), Pain disability (PDI)
Secondary outcome measures: Psychological distress, Spine flexion""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """Duration of each acupuncture session: 30 minutes
Duration needles were left in place: Body needles 30 minutes; Ear needles 1 week""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency """Frequency: Five times a week for the first two weeks, once a week for the next ten weeks.
Number of sessions: 20 sessions.
Duration of each session: 30 minutes.""" .

tara-kb:S-112625-1410 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-1410" ;
    dc:title "Acupuncture Treatment of Chronic Facial Pain A Controlled Cross-over Trial" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol """First trigeminal division: 3 cun above the outer canthus of the eye, 2 cun below the outer canthus of the eye, 1 cun proximal to the margin of the web between the second and third finger.
Second trigeminal division: 2 cun below the outer canthus of the eye, 3 cun lateral to the corner of the mouth, 1 cun proximal to the margin of the web between the second and third finger.
Third trigeminal division: 2 cun below the outer canthus of the eye, 3 cun lateral to the corner of the mouth, 1 cun proximal to the margin of the web between the third and fourth toe.
For pain in more than one division, points from all affected divisions are used.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Randomized Controlled Trial (RCT)" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails """Control group: Placebo acupuncture group
Description: Patients received sham acupuncture treatments where needles were inserted into areas without acupuncture points, ensuring no needling sensation.
Design: Cross-over design with random assignment to either traditional Chinese acupuncture or placebo acupuncture in different periods.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupoints "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasMappedWesternCondition MONDO:0008599 ;
    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails "2-4 mm (for placebo acupuncture)" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-1410 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-1410 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails "Total participants analyzed: 16; Participants in acupuncture first group: 7; Participants in placebo first group: 9" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails """Needle type: Same number as real treatment
Quantity: Same number as real treatment
Size: Not specified
Location: Areas with no acupuncture points
Penetrating: Yes, to a depth of 2-4 mm
Other details: Designed to avoid needling sensation""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails "The needles were retained for 15 minutes without manipulation. The needling sensation was a key aspect of the real acupuncture treatment, but the method of achieving this sensation is not specified. No other forms of stimulation (e.g., electrical, thermal, laser) are mentioned." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper does not explicitly state the disease or condition in Traditional Chinese Medicine terminology)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Chronic facial pain (including atypical facial pain, atypical trigeminal neuralgia, and typical trigeminal neuralgia)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "Yes, traditional Chinese acupuncture was found to be significantly more effective than placebo acupuncture in reducing pain, as supported by statistical tests (Wilcoxon test and Sign test)." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measure: Reduction in pain intensity as recorded by the patients themselves over a period of 16 weeks, measured by the period index (PI).
Secondary outcome measure: Patients' subjective preferences for either traditional Chinese acupuncture or placebo acupuncture, as determined by interviews at the end of the investigation.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """Duration of each acupuncture session: Not specified
Duration needles were left in place: 15 minutes""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "Daily for 10 days, with each treatment lasting 15 minutes" .

