    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings """Direct quote: "After treatment, 34 cases were clinically cured, 32 experienced an obvious effect, 18 were improved, and 5 had no effect. The obvious effective rate was 74.2%, the total effective rate was 94.4%."
Effectiveness compared to control/sham group: Not specified""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary Outcome Measures:
Clinical Cure: ThePain in the diseasedShoulder disappeared and the range of motion in theShoulder joints returned to normal.
Obvious Effect: ThePain in theShoulder disappeared and there was obvious improvement in the range of motion of theShoulder joints.
Improved: There was an obvious release in thePain of theShoulder and the range of motion in theShoulder joints increased.
No effect: Nothing happened.
Secondary Outcome Measures:
Disease duration and its impact on treatment effectiveness (Table 1).
Comparison of outcomes between frozenShoulder and motionlessShoulder cases (Table 2).
Comparison of effectiveness among different treatment groups (Table 3).""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration "Not specified (the paper does not provide explicit information on the duration of each acupuncture session or how long the needles were left in place during a session)" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "Treatment was administered 3 times each week and 9 times made up one course of treatment." .

tara-kb:S-112625-1469 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-1469" ;
    dc:title "Acupuncture for Chronic Shoulder Pain An Experimental Study with Attention to the Role of Placebo and Hypnotic Susceptibility" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol """1. Ho-ku located at the proximal point of the crease, formed by approximating the thumb and index fingers.
2. Ch'ii-Ch'ih located midway between the lateral epicondyle of the elbow and the lateral end of the anticubital fold.
3. Chii-ku at the angle formed by the scapular spine and the lateral clavicle.
4. Chien-yii anterior to the tip of the acromion.
5. Chien-yii (same as 4, possibly a repetition or error in the paper).
6. Nao-yii in the fossa inferior to the scapular spine at a point on a line drawn as a vertical continuation of the posterior axillary fold.
7. Chien-ch√©n on the vertical line with Nao-yii one inch above the superior end of the posterior axillary fold.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Randomized Controlled Trial (RCT)" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails """Routine care: Used in several studies as a control group for manual acupressure and ear acupressure.
Conventional medicine: Mentioned as a comparison for acupuncture interventions.
Sham acupressure: Used in one study as a control for manual acupressure.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "United States" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupoints "Ho-ku, Chii-Chih, Chii-ku, Chien-yii, Nao-yii, Chien-chén" ;
