tara-kb:S-112625-1473 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-1473" ;
    dc:title "Hemiparetic Shoulder Pain Syndrome Treated with Deep Dry Needling During Early Rehabilitation: A Prospective, Open Label, Randomized Investigation" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol """Muscles treated: supraspinatus, infraspinatus, upper and lower trapezium, levator scapulae, rhomboids, teres major, subscapularis, latissimus dorsi, triceps, pectoralis, and middle, upper deltoid anterior.
Selection based on presence of trigger points (TrPs) within these muscles.
Needle locations determined by location of TrPs within muscles.
No specific traditional acupuncture points or acupoints mentioned.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Randomized Controlled Trial (RCT)" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails "Group 2: Standard rehabilitation treatment alone (without dry needling)" ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "Italy" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupoints "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasMappedWesternCondition HP:0030834,
        MONDO:0005098 ;
    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-1473 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-1473 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails """Total sample size: 101
Group 1 (Dry Needling): 54
Group 2 (Control): 47""" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails "Not applicable (no sham group was used in the study)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails "The stimulation involved manual manipulation through twirling the needles to stimulate muscle proprioceptors and elicit a Deqi response. Needles were also left in place for a period of time. The Deqi response, characterized by sensations of soreness, heaviness, and tingling, was sought as part of the stimulation process." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Hemiparetic shoulder pain syndrome resulting from a cerebrovascular accident (CVA, stroke)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "Yes, the acupuncture intervention was effective compared to the control group based on the primary outcome of pain reduction. The results indicate a significant reduction in pain severity and frequency, improved sleep, and increased rehabilitation compliance in the dry needling group compared to the control group. Statistical significance is noted with p values indicating a significant difference between the groups." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measures: Rivermead Motricity Index (RMI) for motility,Pain Visual AnalogScale (PainVAS) forPain.
Secondary outcome measures:Self report questionnaire for rest andSleep improvements.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """Duration of each session: Needles left in place for about five minutes
Frequency: Four sessions every five to seven days""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "The frequency of treatment was four sittings every five to seven days. The treatments were administered over a period that allowed for assessments on days 6, 10, 16, and 22." .

tara-kb:S-112625-1474 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-1474" ;
    dc:title "Comparison Between Superficial and Deep Acupuncture in the Treatment of the Shoulder's Myofascial Pain: A Randomized and Controlled Study" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol """SI 3 (Houxi): proximal to the head of the fifth metacarpal bone at the extremity of the fold evidenced by the palmar flexion of the fingers.
SI 9 (Jianzhen): posteriorly and inferiorly to the shoulder; with the adduced arm it is 1.5 cm above the axillar fold.
LI 11 (Quchi): in the depression at the lateral extremity of the elbow's transverse fold when the elbow makes a 90 degree angle.
LI 15 (JJianyu): in front and under the acromion at the center of the superior part of the deltoid muscle.
TE 14 (Jianliao): between the acromion and the major tubercle of the humerus when the arm is extended along the side.
TE 15 (Tianliao): half way between the head of the acromion and 14TM.
GV 14 (Dazhui): between the spinal processes of the 7th cervical vertebra and the first thoracic one.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Randomized Controlled Trial (RCT)" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails "The control group received personalized, evidence based physical therapy treatment, which included a clinical examination process, manual therapy techniques, stretching, exercises, and postural recommendations. This group did not receive dry needling." ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupoints "SI 3, SI 9, LI 11, LI 15, TE 14, TE 15, GV 14" ;
