    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "Yes, acupuncture was found to be effective compared to the control group based on the primary outcome of total effective rate. The total effective rate for the treatment group was 97.1% compared to 86.1% for the control group. No statistical significance (p values) were reported." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measures:
Cured: Basic appearance of theSubjectiveSymptoms andSigns in the nervousSystem.
Markedly effective: Obvious improvement ofSubjectiveSymptoms, partial restoration of theSystemicSigns in the 'nervousSystem, and 'elevation of myodynamia by 2 grades.
Effective: Improvement of partialSymptoms andSystemicSigns with myodynamia reaching grade II, but the patients are unable to manage his/her life independently.
Failed: TheSymptoms andSystemicSigns remained theSame as before.
Total effective rate (%)
Secondary outcome measures: NotSpecified""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration "Not specified (the paper does not provide information on the duration of each acupuncture session or how long the needles were left in place)" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "Three courses of treatments with an interval of 5 7 days between courses; specific frequency and duration of individual sessions not specified." .

