    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails "1 to 1.5 cm" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-1567 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-1567 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails """Total participants: 124
Participants analyzed: 116 (100% of intervention group, 83.3% of simulated electroacupuncture group)""" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails """Needle type: Real acupuncture needles
Quantity: Approximately eight needles inserted at each treatment
Size: Not specified
Location: 20 to 30 mm from the selected points of the real electroacupuncture, not on meridians
Penetrating: Yes
Electrical stimulation: Inactivated
Duration: Average of 30 minutes (range: 20 60 minutes)""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails "The stimulation was electrical, using an electroacupuncture machine with frequencies of 2 Hz to 5 Hz and an intensity of 1.5 volts. The needles were inserted to a depth of 1 to 1.5 cm and stimulated for 30 minutes. No other forms of stimulation were mentioned." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper does not explicitly translate whiplash associated disorders into Traditional Chinese Medicine terminology)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Whiplash associated disorders (WAD)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings """The study found that real electroacupuncture significantly reduced pain intensity compared to simulated electroacupuncture at both 3 and 6 months (P = 0.05 and P = 0.007).
There was no significant reduction in disability or improvement in health related quality of life.
The reduction in pain intensity was less than the suggested thresholds for clinically important changes.
The effects of acupuncture on pain, disability, and quality of life were moderate and may not be considered clinically relevant.
Yes, the acupuncture intervention was effective compared to the control/sham group for pain intensity, but not for disability or quality of life.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measures:
Pain intensity (10 cm visual analogScale)
Disability (Neck Disability Index)
Health related quality of life (SF 36)
Secondary outcome measures:
PatientSpecific activityScales
McGillPain Rating Index
Restriction in activities of daily living (ADL) measured by VASs
Pain Rating Index Total of theShort Form McGillPain Questionnaire""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """Duration of each acupuncture session: 30 minutes
Needles left in place during a session: 30 minutes""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "Twice weekly for 6 weeks, with 12 sessions in total. Each session lasted approximately 30 minutes (range: 20 60 minutes)." .

