Quantity: 4 needles
Size: Diameter of 0.22 mm, length of 1.5 mm
Location: Non-acupuncture points on the auricular helix
Penetration: Yes, invasive procedure""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails "The stimulation was performed manually by massage of the AA needles. The anesthesiologists stimulated the needles for 5 minutes during specific phases of surgery, and patients were instructed to stimulate the needles for 5 minutes when experiencing pain. No other form of stimulation was mentioned." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper focuses on postoperative pain management rather than a specific TCM disease or condition)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Postoperative pain after total hip arthroplasty" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "Yes, the acupuncture intervention was effective compared to the control/sham group in reducing postoperative analgesic requirements, with statistical significance (p-values: P<0.004 for piritramide requirement, P<0.002 for total piritramide adjusted to weight, P<0.04 for time to first request)." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measure: Postoperative piritramide requirement via PCA during the first 36 h after surgery.
Secondary outcome measures:
Pain intensity on VAS-100 at rest during 3 days after surgery.
Time to first PCA request.
Total piritramide requirement via PCA during 3 days after surgery.
Incidence of analgesia-related side effects (nausea, vomiting, sedation, pruritus).
Total ibuprofen consumption during the second and third postoperative days.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """Duration of each acupuncture session: Not specified
Length of time needles were left in place: 3 days""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "The auricular acupuncture treatment involved the insertion of permanent press AA needles at specific points, which were retained in situ for 3 days after surgery. The needles were inserted once, in the evening before surgery, and patients were instructed to stimulate them by massage as needed. There was no repeated administration of treatments beyond the initial insertion." .

