    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-1607 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails """Total participants: 20
Protocol 1: 20
Protocol 2: 7""" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails """Needle type: Real needles used for sham procedure
Quantity: Four non-acupuncture points
Size: Depth of 0.5 cm
Location: Non-acupuncture points located 10-20 mm lateral to PC5, PC6, ST36, ST37 and not on a meridian line
Penetrating: Yes, but to a shallower depth than active EA
Other details: No manual manipulation or de-qi sensations induced; identical sounds generated but no electrical stimulation delivered""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails """Active EA: Electrical stimulation with a 2 Hz continuous wave current; manual rotation of needles to induce de-qi sensations.
Sham EA: No electrical stimulation; no manual manipulation; no de-qi sensations induced.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper does not explicitly state a primary medical condition or disease in TCM terms)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Osteoarthritis (OA)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "Yes, the acupuncture intervention was effective compared to the control/sham group in preventing endothelial dysfunction as measured by FMD. The sham group showed a significant decrease in FMD after IR injury (p < 0.001), while the EA group did not (p = NS), and EA restored FMD compared to sham (p < 0.05). The use of celecoxib with EA blunted FMD, indicating a COX-2-dependent mechanism (p = 0.001)." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measure: Analgesic effect of acupuncture as determined by subjective ratings of pain before and after treatment.
Secondary outcome measure: Correlation between specific sensations (numbness and soreness) and analgesia.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration "20 minutes (duration of each acupuncture session); Not specified (how long the needles were left in place)" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "Not specified (the paper does not provide information on the frequency of acupuncture treatments beyond the single session per participant with a week in between)" .

