tara-kb:S-112625-1661 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-1661" ;
    dc:title "Clinical observation on effect of scalp electroacupuncture for mild cognitive impairment" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol """Scalp electroacupuncture group: Baihui (DU 20), Sishecong (EX-HN1), Fengchi (GB 20), Shenting (DU 24)
Syndrome differentiation group: Additional points based on TCM syndromes:
Kidney essence deficiency syndrome: Taixi (KI 3)
Phlegm blocking meridian syndrome: Fenglong (ST 40)
Blood stasis blocking meridian syndrome: Xuehai (SP 10)
Liver-Yang hyperactivity syndrome: Taichong (LR 3)
Qi and blood deficiency syndrome: Zusanli (ST 36)
Insertion technique: Needles inserted subgaleally along the scalp at an angle of 15¬∞-30¬∞.
Electroacupuncture parameters: Continuous wave, frequency 3-15 Hz, intensity 2-4 mA.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Randomized Controlled Trial (RCT)" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails """Sham acupuncture: Needles inserted into non-acupuncture points or dummy needles used.
Waiting list control: Participants did not receive any intervention during the study period.
Nicotine replacement therapy (NRT): Used as a control group to compare the effectiveness of acupuncture.
Psychological interventions: Counseling and other psychological approaches used as control groups.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "China" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupoints "GV 20, EX-HN1, GB 20, DU 24, KI 3, ST 40, SP 10, LR 3, ST 36" ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupointsMapped TARA:2324295,
        TARA:3386254,
        TARA:4362146,
        TARA:6463344,
        TARA:6499349,
        TARA:7679931,
        TARA:8433932,
        TARA:9531666,
        TARA:9610280 ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupointsMappedCurie "{\"TARA:3386254\": \"GV 20\", \"TARA:9610280\": \"Sishencong\", \"TARA:2324295\": \"GB 20\", \"TARA:7679931\": \"GV 24\", \"TARA:4362146\": \"KI 3\", \"TARA:9531666\": \"ST 40\", \"TARA:8433932\": \"SP 10\", \"TARA:6499349\": \"LR 3\", \"TARA:6463344\": \"ST 36\"}"^^rdf:JSON ;
    tara-kb:hasMappedWesternCondition HP:0100543 ;
    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails """Fengchi (GB 20): 1.7-4.0 cm
Shenting (DU 24), Sishencong (EX-HN1), Baihui (DU 20): Not specified""" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-1661 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-1661 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails """Total studies: 38
Acupuncture vs. waiting list control: 393 participants
Acupuncture vs. sham acupuncture: 1,588 participants
Acupressure vs. sham acupressure: 253 participants
Continuous auricular stimulation vs. sham stimulation: 1,155 participants
Continuous acupressure: 496 participants
Acupuncture with indwelling needles: 659 participants
Electrostimulation vs. sham electrostimulation (short-term): 634 participants
Electrostimulation vs. sham electrostimulation (long-term): 405 participants""" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails "Sham acupuncture involves inserting needles into areas not recognized as active points or using points believed to be ineffective for the condition. Dummy needles that do not penetrate the skin are also used. Non-penetrating sham needles have been developed but not used in smoking cessation research." ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails "Needles were stimulated by hand or using an electric current (electroacupuncture). Electroacupuncture involved electrical stimulation to release neurotransmitters, sometimes causing a mild tingling sensation. Low-level laser therapy was also mentioned, which does not produce a sensation. No mention of thermal stimulation (moxibustion)." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Mild cognitive impairment (MCI) is associated with TCM syndromes such as kidney deficiency, Qi and blood deficiency, phlegm, blood stasis, and hyperactivity of liver Yang." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Mild Cognitive Impairment (MCI)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "No" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measures: mini-mental state examination (MMSE), picture recognition, clock drawing test
Secondary outcome measures: Not specified""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """Duration of each acupuncture session: often lasting 15 to 20 minutes
Duration needles were left in place: not specified for standard sessions; indwelling needles left in place for several days""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "The frequency of acupuncture treatment varied across studies. Some examples include treatments on days 0, 7, and 28, and twice weekly for two weeks. Indwelling needles may be used for continuous stimulation over several days. Overall, the frequency is not consistently specified across all studies." .

