    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupoints "MS 1, MS 5, MS 11" ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupointsUnmappable "MS 1",
        "MS 11",
        "MS 5" ;
    tara-kb:hasMappedWesternCondition MONDO:0002039 ;
    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails "1.2 cun" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-1676 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-1676 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails """Total participants: 66
Treatment group: 34
Control group: 32""" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails "Not applicable (no sham acupuncture group was used)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails """Stimulation zones: Middle Line of Forehead (MS 1), Middle Line of Vertex (MS 5), Posterior Temporal Line (MS 11) on the affected side.
Needles were stimulated using electrical stimulation (electroacupuncture) with a low frequency electronic treatment instrument at 3.3 Hz and continuous wave.
Manual stimulation: Twirling manipulation before electrical stimulation.
Retention time: 30 minutes.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Stroke (Zhong Feng) in Traditional Chinese Medicine (TCM)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Cognitive Disorder Due to Cerebral Infarction" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings """"After treatment, the latency of P300 was shortened by 39.1 ms in the treatment group, versus 16.7 ms in the control group, and the difference was statistically significant (P<0.05)."
"The total effective rate was 94.1% in the treatment group, versus 43.8% in the control group, and the difference was statistically significant (P<0.05)."
"This study showed that scalp acupuncture had positive effect in restoring the cognitive function of the patients after cerebral infarction."
"According to the measurement of P300 in 66 subjects, the current study suggests that scalp acupuncture has stable effect in treating cognitive impairment due to cerebral infarction, and P300 can effectively reflect the improvement of cognitive function after scalp acupuncture."
Yes""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measures: Latency and amplitude ofP300, total effective rate
Secondary outcome measures: WAISScores""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration "30 minutes (duration retained)" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "The treatment was given once each day, 10 times as a treatment course, with an interval of 3 5 days between each two courses, and 6 courses were given in total." .

tara-kb:S-112625-1677 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-1677" ;
    dc:title "Analgesic effect of acupuncture needle penetration: a double blind crossover study" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol "LI4 point (the largeintestine meridianthe most important analgesic point) on the right hand" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Double blind crossover study" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails """Nonpenetrating (placebo) needle: Used as a control to compare with the penetrating needle.
Noacupuncture control condition: Participants did not receive any needle treatment, used to establish a baseline for pain intensity.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "Japan" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupoints "LI 4" ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupointsMapped TARA:0859695 ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupointsMappedCurie "{\"TARA:0859695\": \"LI 4\"}"^^rdf:JSON ;
    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails "10 mm for penetrating needles; non penetrating needles do not penetrate the skin" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-1677 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-1677 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails """Total sample size: 56
Gender distribution: 31 men, 25 women
Group distribution: Not specified""" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails """Sham procedure: Nonpenetrating needles with a blunt tip that pressed against the skin without penetrating it.
Needle type: Nonpenetrating (placebo) needles.
Quantity: Not specified.
Size: Diameter of 0.16 mm.
Location: Not specified if real points or non points/off meridian.
Penetration: Non penetrating.
Other details: Designed to match appearance and feel of penetrating needles; contained stuffing to simulate skin puncture sensation.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails "The stimulation in this study was electrical stimulation applied to the forearm to elicit pain, used to assess pain intensity before, during, and after needle insertion and removal. The needles themselves were not stimulated with any additional methods." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper does not explicitly mention a specific disease or condition in TCM terms)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Not specified (the study focuses on pain management rather than a specific Western medical condition or disease)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings """"We found no significant difference in analgesic effects between the penetrating and nonpenetrating needle trials."
"A significant analgesic effect was observed during needle application and immediately after needle removal for both the penetrating and nonpenetrating needle trials when compared with the no acupuncture control condition (penetrating v. control: immediately, 10 minutes and 20 minutes after needle insertion [p < 0.001 for each] and immediately, 10 minutes and 20 minutes after needle removal [p < 0.050] for eachÕæ nonpenetrating v. control: immediately, 10 minutes and 20 minutes after needle insertion [p < 0.001 for each] and immediately after needle removal [p = 0.010])."
"Interpretation: Needle penetration did not confer a specific analgesic advantage over nonpenetrating (placebo) needle application."
"In this double blind study, we found that the analgesic effect from the skin penetration and deep needle insertion (a distinctive feature of acupuncture) with the penetrating needle was no greater than the analgesic effect from the skin pressure alone with the nonpenetrating needle."
"Furthermore, we found no significant correlation between the analgesic effect and de qi, which has been considered essential for acupuncture analgesia."
The acupuncture intervention was effective compared to the control/sham group(s) based on the primary outcome(s): Yes""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measure: Pain elicited when electricalStimulation was applied to the posterior forearm.
Secondary outcome measures: Pain fromSkin penetration and de qi (deep dull pain) associated with needle application.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration "20 minutes" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "Not specified (the paper does not provide explicit information on the frequency of treatment sessions beyond the crossover design and the interval between sessions)" .

tara-kb:S-112625-1678 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-1678" ;
    dc:title "An innovative acupuncture treatment for primary dysmenorrhea: a randomized cross over pilot study" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol """Spleen 6 (SP6) acupuncture point: Located on the lower leg, three cun proximal to the peak of the medial malleolus, along the posterior aspect of the tibial bone.
Non acupuncture control point: Located 1 cun medial to the Liver channel and 5 cun above the medial malleolus.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Pilot Study with Crossover Design" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails "Saline injection at a non acupuncture point located 1 cun medial to the Liver channel and 5 cun above the medial malleolus." ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "United States" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupoints "SP 6" ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupointsMapped TARA:4385250 ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupointsMappedCurie "{\"TARA:4385250\": \"SP 6\"}"^^rdf:JSON ;
    tara-kb:hasMappedWesternCondition MONDO:1060206 ;
    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails "2.5 to 3 cm deep" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-1678 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-1678 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails """Total participants enrolled: 20
Total participants randomized: 15
Total participants completing all study visits: 14
Participants within groups: Not specified""" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails """Sham procedure: Saline injection
Location: Non acupuncture point located 1 cun medial to the Liver channel and 5 cun above the medial malleolus
Method: Intramuscular injection
Needle type: Identical to those used for vitamin K1 injection
Quantity and size: Not specified
Penetrating: Yes, intramuscular injection""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails "Not applicable (the study involved acupuncture point injection without needle stimulation)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper does not provide a specific TCM term for the condition)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Primary dysmenorrhea" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "Yes, the acupuncture intervention was found to be effective compared to the control/sham group based on the primary outcome of pain intensity reduction. The study reported a statistically significant reduction in pain intensity after vitamin K1 injection compared to saline, with a 2.5 point decrease versus a 1.8 point decrease, respectively. The change scores between vitamin K1 and saline approached statistical significance. The reduction in pain intensity is considered clinically significant based on the 11 point NRS scale. However, larger sample sizes are needed to confirm statistical significance and clinical meaningfulness compared to saline." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary Outcome Measure: DysmenorrheaPain intensity measured using a 0 10 numeric ratingScale before and after injections.
Secondary Outcome Measures:
Cox RetrospectiveSymptomScale
Moos Menstrual Distress Questionnaire
Activity restrictions
Use of analgesic and rescue medications
Adverse events""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration "Not specified (the paper does not provide specific duration for each acupuncture session or how long the needles were left in place)" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "The frequency of treatment was monthly, with injections administered within the first two days of menstruation. Each participant received two treatments, with a washout period of at least two months between them." .

