tara-kb:S-112625-1704 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-1704" ;
    dc:title "Acupuncture for the relief of cancer-related pain -a systematic review" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol """Xia et al. (1986): P6, ST36, and other points according to symptoms
Filshie et al. (1996): LI4 or ST36 and two studs on sternum
Xu et al. (1995): ST36 bilaterally
Alimi et al. (2003) and Alimi et al. (2000): Individualized ear acupuncture (points not specified)""" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType """Randomized Controlled Trials (RCTs): Xia et al. (1986), Dang and Yang (1998), Alimi et al. (2003)
Uncontrolled studies: Rico and Trudnowski (1982), Xu et al. (1995), Filshie et al. (1996), Alimi et al. (2000)""" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails """Conventional therapies (Xia et al., 1986; Dang and Yang, 1998)
Placebo acupuncture (Alimi et al., 2003)""" ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "China, France, United Kingdom, United States" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasMappedWesternCondition HP:0012531 ;
    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-1704 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-1704 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails "Not specified (the paper does not provide explicit sample size numbers for the total participants or within groups)" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails """Sham procedure: Ear acupuncture at non-acupuncture points
Needle type: Not specified
Quantity and size: Not specified
Location: Non-acupuncture points
Penetrating or non-penetrating: Not specified""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails """Manual manipulation: The manipulation of acupuncture needles is suggested to stimulate the release of endorphins and enkephalins.
Electrical stimulation (electroacupuncture): Mentioned as a form of acupuncture used in one study.
De-Qi elicited: A sensation sought during acupuncture treatment in the Xu et al. (1995) study.
Not specified: Exact method of stimulation for each study beyond general terms.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Cancer-related pain" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings """The paper concludes that the current evidence does not support acupuncture as an effective analgesic adjunct for cancer patients.
One high-quality randomized clinical trial showed statistically significant pain relief with ear acupuncture compared to placebo ear acupuncture (P = 0.02 and P < 0.001).
The effectiveness of acupuncture compared to control/sham groups is generally inconclusive due to methodological flaws in most studies.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measures: Pain intensity on VAS, patient's verbal assessment, plasma leucine-enkephalin level
Secondary outcome measures: Not specified""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration "Not specified (the paper does not provide specific information on the duration of each acupuncture session or how long the needles were left in place during a session)" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "The frequency of treatment varied across studies: one study involved a two-month body acupuncture treatment, another a two-week treatment, and one study involved a single session. Specific frequencies for other studies are not detailed in the text but may be found in the tables. Overall, the frequency is not consistently specified across all studies." .

