Second trial (Zhou 2002): 90 (48/42)""" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails "Not applicable (no sham acupuncture group details provided)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails """Scalp and body acupuncture: Manual manipulation using "even supplementation and drainage" techniques until the arrival of Qi.
Dermal needle therapy: Tapping vertically with an interval of 1cm.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper does not provide a specific TCM term for RLS)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Restless legs syndrome (RLS)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "Maybe/Inconclusive (one trial showed no significant difference, another showed some effectiveness with dermal needle therapy, but overall evidence is insufficient)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measures: Unpleasant sensations of RLS measured by any type of validated scale (e.g., VAS)
Improvement of overall symptoms measured as a dichotomous outcome (remission versus no remission)
Secondary outcome measures: Periodic leg movements during sleep (PLMS) index
Absolute or percentage reduction in RLS frequency and duration
Sleep disturbance measured on a scale (e.g., sleep onset latency)
Wakefulness after sleep onset (WASO) or by reported total sleep time
Daytime functioning
Quality of life measures (e.g., SF-36)
Frequency and types of adverse effects""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """Duration of treatment: 24 days (8 days x 3 courses)
Duration of each acupuncture session: 30 minutes
Duration of dermal needle sessions: Not specified""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "Acupuncture was administered daily for eight days, followed by a two-day interval, repeated over three treatment courses. Needles were retained for 30 minutes with manipulation twice during this time. Both groups received treatment for 30 days consecutively." .

