tara-kb:S-112625-1725 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-1725" ;
    dc:title "Does Traditional Chinese Medicine Pattern Affect Acupoint Specific Effect? Analysis of Data from a Multicenter, Randomized, Controlled Trial for Primary Dysmenorrhea" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol """Sanyinjiao (SP6): located at the posterior border of the medial aspect of tibia, at 3 cun above the medial malleolus
Xuanzhong (GB39): located at the anterior of fibula, at 3 cun above the tip of the external malleolus
Adjacent nonacupoint: located on the lateral side of lower part of the leg, 3 inches above the tip of external malleolus, 1.5 inches behind anterior crest of the tibia""" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Randomized Controlled Trial (RCT)" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails """GB39 group: Used as an unrelated acupoint control.
Nonacupoint group: Used as a control to assess the effect of needling at a non-acupuncture point.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "China" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupoints "SP 6, GB 39, adjacent nonacupoint" ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupointsMapped TARA:3770332,
        TARA:4385250 ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupointsMappedCurie "{\"TARA:4385250\": \"SP 6\", \"TARA:3770332\": \"GB 39\"}"^^rdf:JSON ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupointsUnmappable "adjacent nonacupoint" ;
    tara-kb:hasMappedWesternCondition MONDO:1060206 ;
    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails "25-40 mm" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-1725 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-1725 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails """Total participants enrolled: 501
Total participants analyzed with single TCM patterns: 323
Participants within TCM patterns:
Cold and Dampness Stagnation pattern: 184
Qi and Blood Stagnation pattern: 84
Qi and Blood Deficiency pattern: 52""" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails """Sham acupuncture treatments were performed at two locations: Xuanzhong (GB39) and an adjacent nonacupoint.
GB39 is an unrelated acupoint used as a control.
The nonacupoint is located on the lateral side of the lower part of the leg.
Both sham procedures involved bilateral electroacupuncture with real needles.
The sham procedures were penetrating but not intended to have a therapeutic effect.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails "Electrical stimulation (electroacupuncture) was used. Needles were stimulated with alternating 2/100-Hz waves at the highest intensity each participant could tolerate. Needling techniques of lifting-thrusting and twirling were used to induce a needling sensation (de qi). The stimulation lasted 30 minutes." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Primary dysmenorrhea (PD)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "Yes, for Cold and Dampness Stagnation pattern; No, for Qi and Blood Stagnation and Qi and Blood Deficiency patterns; Not clinically relevant despite statistical significance." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measure: Immediate improvement of pain measured with a 100-mm VAS before, during, and after the intervention.
Secondary outcome measure: Not specified.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration "30 minutes (duration of each acupuncture session), 30 minutes (duration needles were left in place during a session)" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "Not specified (the paper only mentions a single treatment session lasting 30 minutes on the first day of menstruation, but does not provide information on the frequency of treatments beyond this session)" .

