tara-kb:S-112625-1733 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-1733" ;
    dc:title "Effects of dry needling to the symptomatic versus control shoulder in patients with unilateral subacromial pain syndrome *" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol """Needle locations: Superior, medial, and inferior parts of the infraspinatus muscle.
Related acupoints: Not specified (treatment focused on myofascial trigger points rather than traditional acupuncture points).""" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Within-subjects controlled trial" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails """Placebo EA: Used in electroacupuncture studies as a sham control.
Minimal acupuncture: Superficial needling in non-acupoint locations.
No intervention: Used in studies comparing acupuncture to no treatment.
Conventional analgesia: Used in studies comparing acupuncture to standard medical pain relief.
TENS: Used in studies comparing acupuncture to another form of pain relief.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "United States" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupoints "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasMappedWesternCondition HP:0030834 ;
    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails "Not specified (the paper does not provide explicit information on the depth of needle insertion)" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-1733 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-1733 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails """Total sample size: 2038
Range of participants per study: 90 to 607
Specific sample sizes within groups are detailed in the tables at the end of the paper.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails """Placebo EA: Simulated needle penetration, followed by needle fixing on an acupoint with adhesive tapes and connected to an inactive electric device (non-penetrating).
Minimal acupuncture: Superficial needling in places not regarded as acupoints, such as buttocks, thighs, and arms (penetrating at non-acupoints).""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails "Electroacupuncture (EA) involved electrical stimulation. Minimal acupuncture involved superficial needling without traditional stimulation. The paper does not specify manual manipulation, thermal, or laser stimulation, nor does it mention if any sensation was sought." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper does not explicitly mention the condition in Traditional Chinese Medicine terminology)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Unilateral subacromial pain syndrome" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "No" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measure: Penn Shoulder Score
Secondary outcome measures: Ultrasonic measures of infraspinatus muscle thickness, pressure algometry, shoulder internal rotation and horizontal adduction ROM, Global Rating of Change, Numerical Pain Rating Scale""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """Ramner√∂ (2002): Needles removed after 1-3 hours or when necessary.
Skilnand (2002): Needles removed after 20 minutes or left until conversion to conventional analgesia or delivery.
Ziaei (2006): Needles left until delivery.
Zhang (2006): 30 minutes.
Zhou (2007): 30 minutes.
Borup (2009): Needles left for 30 minutes to 2 hours or repeated as necessary.
Hantoushzadeh (2007): Needles removed if necessary.
Huang (2008): Not specified.
M√•rtensson (2008): Needles left for 40 minutes or repeated if necessary.
Nesheim (2003): Needles removed after 10-20 minutes or left in place for the entire duration of labour.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "Not specified (the paper provides details on the duration and timing of individual acupuncture sessions during labor but does not specify the frequency of treatment outside of this context)" .

