    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "Not applicable (the paper is a protocol and does not include results or conclusions)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measure: Impact of electroacupuncture on AMH concentration in women with PCOS.
Secondary outcome measures: Correlation of baseline AMH with ovulation and menstrual cycle frequencies; comparison of AMH levels between PCOS women and a large cohort of fertility clinic patients; lack of difference in AMH levels between true and sham acupuncture arms.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """Duration of each acupuncture session: Not specified
Needle retention time: 11 minutes""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "Three sessions in one day, with a 30-minute washout period between each session." .

tara-kb:S-112625-1775 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-1775" ;
    dc:title "Factors Associated with Conflicting Findings on Acupuncture for Tension-Type Headache: Qualitative and Quantitative Analyses" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol """One study used electroacupuncture with distal points only.
Other studies used manual acupuncture with both local and distal points.
Specific acupoints mentioned include: GB20, LI4, LR3, GB8, GB14, GB21, GB41, UB2, UB10, BL60, LU7, TE5, ST8, ST36, ST44, GV20, Extra1 (Yintang)
Formulas used in some studies: LI 4; SJ 5; ST40; GB60; LR 3; LR 2; LI 4; LU 7; SP 6; ST 36; LI 4; SJ 5; LR 3; LI 4; KI 3; SJ 5. Some studies used individualized treatment based on TCM diagnosis, while others used fixed points.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Randomized Controlled Trial (RCT)" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails "Sham acupuncture was used as the control group in the studies included in this review. Sham acupuncture involved using nonacupuncture points and applying the same needle retention time as in real acupuncture. Some studies used an invasive method with superficial insertion onto nonpoints without de qi, while others used a noninvasive method." ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "Germany, United Kingdom, Australia" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasMappedWesternCondition HP:0012228 ;
    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-1775 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-1775 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails """Total participants: 838
Sample size range: 40 to 409
Gender distribution: More females than males (specific numbers not provided)
Age range: 30 to 50 years (specific numbers not provided)""" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails """Sham procedure used non-acupuncture points.
Same needle retention time as real acupuncture.
Some studies used invasive method with superficial insertion into non-points without de qi.
Others used a non-invasive method.
Specific details on needle type, quantity, and size are not provided in the text but are likely detailed in Table 4.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails """Electroacupuncture (EA) was used in one study, involving electrical stimulation.
Manual acupuncture (MA) was used in the other studies, likely involving manual manipulation of the needles.
Some studies aimed to achieve a "de qi" sensation.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper does not provide TCM terminology for the condition)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Tension-type headache (TTH)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "No" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measure: Headache days
Secondary outcome measures: VAS, frequency of headache, GHQ, SF-36, CGI, ELQ, LS, D-S, NHP, PTH, HDI, SIP, pain disability index, SES, ADS SF-36, SF-12, Von Korff chronic pain grade scale, global patient assessment""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """Needle retention time: 30 minutes for four studies
Needle retention time: 15 seconds for one study
Duration of each acupuncture session: Not specified""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency """Treatment sessions ranged from 8 to 15 over 4-8 weeks.
Frequency: Weekly or twice a week.
Four studies had twice-a-week treatment sessions.
One study had weekly treatment sessions.""" .

