tara-kb:S-112625-1808 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-1808" ;
    dc:title "The Use of Auricular Acupuncture in Opioid Use Disorder: A Systematic Literature Review" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol "Sympathetic, Shen men, Lung, Kidney, Liver" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Randomized Controlled Trial (RCT)" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails """One trial used mock AA as a control condition.
One trial used methadone maintenance treatment (MMT) alone as a control condition.
Two trials used sham acupuncture as a control condition.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "Not specified (the paper does not explicitly state the country or countries where the studies were conducted)" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasMappedWesternCondition MONDO:0002491 ;
    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-1808 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-1808 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails "Total participants: 350; Specific group sizes: Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails "Sham acupuncture was performed at supposedly inactive acupoints. No further details on needle type, quantity, size, or whether it was penetrating or non-penetrating are provided." ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails "Manual manipulation (as part of manual AA)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper does not provide a TCM equivalent for opioid use disorder)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Opioid use disorder (OUD)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings """The two RCTs that reported on craving did not find the addition of AA to MAT beneficial.
Two of three trials did not find any additional benefit of AA in reducing withdrawal symptoms.
One trial reported that adjunctive AA allowed for a significant dose reduction.
One trial reported a statistically significant positive effect of AA on treatment retention.
The one trial exploring psychosocial and HRQoL outcomes reported a significantly positive impact of AA.
While AA may not be beneficial in decreasing craving or withdrawal symptoms, it may allow for reduced methadone doses, aid in treatment retention and completion, and be a useful adjunct to address psychosocial aspects of recovery.
Effectiveness: Maybe/Inconclusive""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measures: opiate dependence, craving, treatment retention
Secondary outcome measures: withdrawal, cigarette consumption, HRQoL, AA acceptability""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration "Not specified (the paper does not provide information on the duration of each acupuncture session or how long the needles were left in place)" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency """Inpatient setting: 5 days per week
Outpatient setting: 3 to 7 days per week
Duration and setting: Similar across all trials, but exact duration not specified""" .

