tara-kb:S-112625-1822 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-1822" ;
    dc:title "Subject Expectancy and the Reduction of Cold Pressor Pain with Acupuncture and Placebo Acupuncture" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol """Ho-ku: between the thumb and forefinger
Waikuan: back of the wrist between the ulna and the radius""" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Randomized Controlled Trial (RCT)" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails """Control group: No treatment was administered. Subjects were told that their next ice water dip would be identical to their previous one and lay on a hospital bed for 20 minutes to allow their arm to recover.
Expectancy conditions within the control group: High expectancy (told that lying on the bed would reduce pain), low expectancy (told that lying on the bed would have no effect on pain), and no expectancy (told that the effect of lying on the bed was variable).""" ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "Canada" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified (the paper does not mention any dataset link or data availability statement)" ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupoints "Ho-ku, Waikuan" ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupointsMapped TARA:0859695 ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupointsMappedCurie "{\"TARA:0859695\": \"LI 4\"}"^^rdf:JSON ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupointsUnmappable "Waikuan" ;
    tara-kb:hasMappedWesternCondition HP:0012531 ;
    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails """First needle: approximately 3 cm between the thumb and forefinger (Ho-ku)
Second needle: 1.3 -1.8 cm into the back of the wrist between the ulna and the radius (Waikuan)""" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-1822 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-1822 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails """Total sample size: 72 subjects
Main groups: Acupuncture (24), Placebo acupuncture (24), Control (24)
Expectancy conditions within each group: High (8), Low (8), No expectancy (8)""" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails """Needle type: Real 28 gauge stainless steel needles
Quantity: Two needles
Size: 28 gauge
Location: Inserted into the opposite arm from the one being tested for pain, at points close to traditional acupuncture points but not themselves acupuncture points
Penetrating: Yes, needles were inserted into the skin
Other details: The sham procedure involved a rationale that the effect is contralateral, and the frequency of electrical stimulation was lower than that used in the real acupuncture procedure.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails "The stimulation was through electroacupuncture using an Acupuncture Anesthesia Apparatus that delivered peak-to-peak trapezoidal wave pulses. The pulse frequency varied between the acupuncture and placebo groups. No other types of stimulation were mentioned." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper does not mention a specific disease or condition in TCM terminology)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Cold pressor pain" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "Yes, acupuncture was effective in reducing pain when combined with high expectancy manipulation, as indicated by significant pain relief (p < 0.01) compared to baseline and other groups." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measure: Numerical rating of pain intensity at 30 seconds in ice water.
Secondary outcome measure: Subjects' comparison of pain experiences on experimental and baseline trials using a 7-point scale.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """Duration of each acupuncture session: approximately 60 minutes
Duration needles were left in place: approximately 20 minutes""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "Not specified (the paper only describes a single experimental session with one treatment administration)" .

