    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails """Type 1: No penetration into the skin using the Park sham needle, which has a round and blunt needle head that retracts into the handle and does not penetrate the skin.
Type 2: Slight penetration into the skin or press.
Type 3: Stimulation of nonacupoint parts, which may still have therapeutic effects.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails "Not specified (the paper does not explicitly mention the type of stimulation used for the needles)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "- Constipation - Chronic neuralgia - Infantile colic - Nausea and vomiting during delivery - Malposition - Lumbago and backache - Knee osteoarthritis - Knee joint pain of postmenopausal women - Emergency treatment - Irritable bowel syndrome - Woman health - Polycystic ovarian syndrome - Emesis - Low back pain - Smoking cessation - Atrial fibrillation - Postmenopausal symptoms and reproductive hormones - Cancer-related fatigue - Chronic low back pain - Migraine attacks - Obesity - Inner ear originated tinnitus - Bronchial asthma - Premature ejaculation - Osteoarthritis of the knee" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings """The primary basis for negative results or placebo effects in acupuncture trials is not due to methodological quality but rather noncompliance with acupuncture theory requirements.
The studies with negative results or placebo effects had several intervention-related deficiencies.
The placebo control methods may have therapeutic effects, leading to false-negative results.
The effectiveness of acupuncture compared to control/sham groups is not explicitly stated in terms of statistical significance or primary outcomes.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure "Not specified (the paper does not explicitly mention primary or secondary outcome measures for assessing treatment effectiveness)" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration "Not specified (the paper does not provide explicit information on the duration of each acupuncture session or how long the needles were left in place)" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency """For studies with negative results or placebo effects: 47% had a treatment frequency of 1-2 times/week; 53% had a frequency of ‚â•3 times/week.
For studies with positive results: 35% had a treatment frequency of 1-2 times/week; 65% had a frequency of ‚â•3 times/week.
Optimal frequency suggested: 1-2 times per day.""" .

