    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Menopause-related symptoms in breast cancer survivors" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings """Acupuncture had small-size effects on reducing hot-flash frequency and the severity of menopause-related symptoms.
The pooled effect size for hot-flash frequency was j0.23 (95% CI, j0.44 to j0.006; P = .04).
The effect of true acupuncture was not significantly different from sham acupuncture for hot-flash frequency and severity.
Acupuncture significantly improved the severity of menopause-related symptoms (g = j0.56; 95% CI, j1.040 to j0.09; P = .02).
The effects on hot-flash frequency were short-term and did not persist at 1 to 3 months' follow-up.
Acupuncture was effective in reducing menopause-related symptoms but not hot-flash frequency and severity at follow-up.
Effectiveness compared to control/sham: Maybe/Inconclusive""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcomes: Frequency and severity of hot flashes, menopause-related symptoms
Secondary outcomes: Not specified""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration "Not specified (the paper does not provide specific information on the duration of each acupuncture session or the retention time of the needles)" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency """Total treatment sessions: 5 to 15 sessions
Average intervention dose: 283.6 minutes (range: 75 to 450 minutes)
Frequency of treatment: Not specified""" .

