Size: Not specified
Penetration: Penetrating""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails "The stimulation used in the study was electroacupuncture (EA), where needles were stimulated using an acupuncture point nerve stimulator with a dense-dispersed wave, 2 Hz/100 Hz in frequency, alternating every 3 s. The stimulation intensity was at the level of maximal tolerance for each patient, indicating that a sensation was sought. The stimulation lasted from the induction of anesthesia until the end of the operation." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Not specified (the study focuses on immune response during surgery, not a specific disease or condition)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "Yes, the acupuncture intervention was effective compared to the control group in reducing immunosuppression during surgery, as indicated by the maintenance of immune markers. However, there was no significant difference between real and sham EA groups." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measures:
Impact of EA on the dynamic equilibrium of the immune system
Effects of EA on pro-inflammatory and anti-inflammatory cytokines (TNFŒ±, IL-8, IL-10)
Effects of EA on immunoglobulins (IgM, IgA, IgG)
Effects of EA on immune cell populations (neutrophils, monocytes, lymphocytes, lymphocyte subsets)
Secondary outcome measure:
Investigation of point specificity of EA""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration "Not specified (the paper does not provide specific duration or retention time for acupuncture sessions)" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "Not specified (the paper only describes the duration of EA during a single surgical procedure and does not provide information on the frequency of treatments beyond this context)" .

