    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails "Not applicable (no sham acupuncture group was used in the study)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails "The stimulation used in the study was electrical stimulation (electro-acupuncture) using the SDZ-II nerve and muscle stimulator. The stimulation parameters were alternating current with a pulse width of 1 ms at 1 pulse/s. The intensity was adjusted to achieve a minimal throbbing sensation at the needling points." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper does not provide a TCM equivalent or description for chronic plantar fasciitis)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Chronic plantar fasciitis" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "Yes" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measure: Success rate determined by a minimum of a 50% decrease in Visual Analog Scale (VAS)
Secondary outcome measure: Overall manifestation measured by the Foot Function Index (FFI)""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """Duration of each acupuncture session: Not specified
Duration needles were left in place: Not specified (stimulation lasted for 30 minutes)""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "Ten sessions of electro-acupuncture, administered twice weekly, with each session lasting 30 minutes." .

tara-kb:S-112625-224 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-224" ;
    dc:title "Effectiveness of Trigger Point Dry Needling for Plantar Heel Pain: A Randomized Controlled Trial" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol "Soleus, Gastrocnemius, Quadratus plantae, Flexor digitorum brevis, Abductor hallucis, Piriformis, Gluteus maximus, Gluteus medius, Gluteus minimus, Tensor fascia latae, Adductor longus, Adductor magnus, Adductor brevis, Semitendinosus, Semimembranosus, Biceps femoris" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Randomized Controlled Trial (RCT)" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails "Sham dry needling group: Participants received sham trigger point dry needling using nonpenetrating sham acupuncture needles, with simulated insertion and manipulation to mimic real treatment without actual penetration." ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "Australia" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified (the paper does not mention any dataset link or availability of related datasets)" ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupoints "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasMappedWesternCondition HP:0012531 ;
    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-224 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-224 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails "Total sample size: 84 participants; Distribution within groups: Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails """Needle type: Nonpenetrating sham acupuncture needles
Quantity: Not specified
Size: 50 mm long, 0.30 mm diameter
Location: Overlying the myofascial trigger points (MTrPs)
Penetrating or non-penetrating: Non-penetrating
Other details: Simulated removal and manipulation of the needle; real needle disposal in a sharps container to mimic real procedure""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails "The needles were stimulated through manual manipulation by being withdrawn partially and advanced repeatedly. The aim was to elicit sensations such as aching, soreness, and pressure, and if possible, a local twitch response. The needles were left in situ for 5 minutes after manipulation." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper does not mention Traditional Chinese Medicine terminology)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Plantar heel pain" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "Yes" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measures:
First-step pain measured with a visual analog scale (VAS)
Foot pain measured with the pain subscale of the Foot Health Status Questionnaire (FHSQ)
Secondary outcome measures:
Self-reported magnitude of symptom change using a 15-point Likert scale
Foot posture evaluated using the Foot Posture Index
Perceived credibility and expectations measured by the Credibility/Expectancy Questionnaire (CEQ)
Level of activity documented by the 7-day Physical Activity Recall (PAR) questionnaire
Reporting of adverse events, other cointerventions, pain-relieving medication, and new medical conditions""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """Duration of each acupuncture session: 30 minutes
Duration needles were left in place: 5 minutes""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "The frequency of treatment was one treatment per week for six weeks, with each session lasting 30 minutes. The mean time between treatments was approximately 7 days. Treatments averaged 4 needles per session, each retained for 5 minutes." .

tara-kb:S-112625-225 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-225" ;
    dc:title "How effective is acupuncture for reducing pain due to How effective is acupuncture for reducing pain due to plantar fasciitis? plantar fasciitis?" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol """Daling (PC7): Located on the palmar side of the forearm at the midpoint of the wrist crease.
Hegu (LI4): Located between the first and second metacarpal bones.
Plantar fascia sites: Used in some studies, but specific points not specified.
Karagounis et al: Used up to 12 acupoints from a list of 20, including both plantar fascia and distal acupoints.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Not applicable (the paper is a systematic review)" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails """Standard treatment: ice, stretching, strengthening exercises, and prefabricated insoles
Sham acupuncture: needling at the acupoint Hegu (LI4)
Prefabricated insoles only
Oral analgesics and NSAIDs (diclofenac) as part of standard therapy""" ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupoints "PC 7, LI 4" ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupointsMapped TARA:0859695,
        TARA:9020892 ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupointsMappedCurie "{\"TARA:9020892\": \"PC 7\", \"TARA:0859695\": \"LI 4\"}"^^rdf:JSON ;
    tara-kb:hasMappedWesternCondition MONDO:0004833 ;
    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-225 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-225 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails """Ebrahim et al, 2007: n = 23 (9 F, 14 M)
Zhang et al, 2011: n = 53 (39 F, 14 M)
Karagounis et al, 2011: n = 38 (0 F, 38 M)
Kumnerddee et al, 2012: n = 30 (27 F, 3 M)""" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails """Sham procedure: Needling at the Hegu (LI4) acupoint, located between the first and second metacarpal bones.
Needle type: Real needles (as adverse reactions like bruising and oedema were reported).
Quantity and size: Not specified.
Location: Non-specific acupoint for heel pain, with general analgesic properties.
Penetration: Penetrating (as adverse reactions indicate needle insertion).""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails """Electro-acupuncture: Application of electrical current to acupuncture needles.
Dry needling: Manual manipulation to achieve 'Deqi' sensation (dull ache, numbness, or heaviness).""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper does not provide TCM terminology for the condition)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Plantar fasciitis" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "Yes, acupuncture was found to be effective in reducing pain compared to control/sham groups in the short term (4-8 weeks), but there is insufficient evidence for long-term effectiveness." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measures: Visual Analogue Scale (VAS), Plantar Fasciitis Pain/Disability Scale (PFPS)
Secondary outcome measures: Adverse reactions (e.g., headaches, dizziness, local oedema)""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration "Not specified (the paper does not provide information on the duration of each acupuncture session or how long the needles were left in place)" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "Not specified (the paper does not provide information on the frequency of acupuncture treatments)" .

tara-kb:S-112625-226 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-226" ;
    dc:title "Acupuncture Treatment for Plantar Fasciitis: A Randomized Controlled Trial with Six Months Follow-Up" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol """PC7 (Daling): Located on the palmar side of the forearm at the midpoint of the wrist crease. Used for pain conditions including stomachache, chest or cardiac pain, headache, and heel pain.
LI4 (Hegu): Located between the first and second metacarpal bones. Has analgesic properties, commonly used for dental pain, headache, and general analgesia.
Needling performed on the contralateral side to the heel pain; bilateral needling if pain was on both sides.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Randomized Controlled Trial (RCT)" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails "The control group received needling at the acupoint Hegu (LI 4), which has analgesic properties and is used as a comparison to assess the specificity of PC7 for heel pain." ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "Hong Kong" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupoints "PC 7, LI 4" ;
