tara-kb:S-112625-277 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-277" ;
    dc:title "Acupuncture to treat common reproductive health complaints: An overview of the evidence" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol """SP4, KD3, ST36, ST30, CV2, CV4, CV6, GV20, LI4, Liv3, CV3, CV4, CV6, PC6, GB34, BL23, auricular point Shenmen.
Abdominal muscles and below the knee (Th12-L2, S2-S4)
CV4, CV6, ST29, SP6, SP9, LI4, PC6
LI4 and PC6 manually stimulated
Five fixed points and additional points based on headache location
A5, A6, A8, A12, A16, A18, Liver Ki Mek N18, Spleen Ki Mek F6""" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Randomized Controlled Trial (RCT)" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails """Sham or placebo control acupuncture
No additional treatment
Standard treatment
Other treatment
Self-care
Waitlist control
Medication
Sham controls
Placebo needles
Superficial sham acupuncture
Sham non-penetrating acupuncture""" ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "Germany, Norway, Croatia, Sweden, Korea" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasMappedWesternCondition MONDO:0004169,
        MONDO:1060205 ;
    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails "15-35 mm" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-277 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-277 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails """Menopause: 309 women across six trials
PCOS: 20 women
Menstrual related headache: 31 women (data complete for 28)
PMS: 35 women in one study, 20 women in another
Dysmenorrhea: 208 women
Total sample size across all studies: Not specified""" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails """Sham procedure: Placebo needles, superficial sham acupuncture, sham non penetrating acupuncture control.
Details: Non-penetrating or minimal penetration; no specific details on needle type, quantity, size, or location.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails """Manual manipulation: LI4 and PC6 were manually stimulated four times every 10 min.
Electro-acupuncture: Other points were stimulated using electro-acupuncture at 2 Hz.
De qi was sought: De qi was obtained if possible, and needle manipulation used an even, reducing or reinforcing technique.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper does not explicitly mention TCM terminology for the conditions studied)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Menopause, Dysmenorrhea, PMS, PCOS, Menstrual-related symptoms" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings """The evidence for acupuncture to treat dysmenorrhea and menopause remains unclear due to small study populations and methodological bias.
Acupuncture to treat PMS, PCOS, and other menstrual related symptoms is under-studied.
Specific studies show positive outcomes: Hot flash frequency and intensity reduced significantly (P < 0.001).
Menstrual pain intensity reduced significantly (P < 0.001).
PMS symptoms reduced significantly.
Overall, the evidence is insufficient to support acupuncture's effectiveness for reproductive health complaints.
Effectiveness compared to control/sham groups: Maybe/Inconclusive""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Menopause: Hot flushes and menopausal symptoms (measured by symptom checklists)
Dysmenorrhea: Pain intensity
PMS: Menstrual Symptom Severity List
Secondary outcomes: Not specified""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """PCOS study: Treatment lasted 30 minutes, given twice a week for two weeks, then spaced to weekly and fortnightly. Needles inserted to a depth of 15-35 mm.
Menopause study: Women received 10 sessions over 12 weeks.
PMS study: Subjects treated every second day for 30 minutes.
Not specified for other studies.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency """Borud et al., 2009: 10 sessions over 12 weeks
Stener-Victorin et al., 2009: Twice weekly for two weeks, then weekly and fortnightly
Witt et al., 2008: Maximum of 15 treatments (frequency not specified)
Linde et al., 2004: Administered on specific days before menstruation
Shin et al., 2009: 10 sessions over four weeks""" .

