tara-kb:S-112625-281 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-281" ;
    dc:title "Acupuncture for Erectile Dysfunction: A Systematic Review" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol """Aydin et al., 1997: True: Qichong (ST30), Zusanli (ST36), Zhaohai (KI6), Guanyuan (CV4), Qihai (CV6); Sham: different points compared to classical acupuncture points
Engelhardt et al., 2003: True: Zhaohai (KI6), Shufu (KI27), Guanyuan (CV4), Qihai (CV6), Wangu (SI4), Sanyinjiao (SP6), Shenshu (BL23); Sham: Xuanzhong (GB39), Jiexi (ST41), Tianshu (ST25) (3 acupoints for headache)
Jiang et al., 2012: Qihai (CV6), Guanyuan (CV4), Zhongji (CV3), Sanyinjiao (SP6), Taichong (LR3), Xingjian (LR2), Taixi (KI3), Yongquan (KI1), Neiguan (PC6), Shenmen (HT7), Baihui (GV20), Xinshu (BL15), Shenshu (BL23), Qihaishu (BL24), Mingmen (GV4)""" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Randomized Controlled Trial (RCT)" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails """Aydin et al.: Sham electroacupuncture (EA) with needles inserted into non-acupoints.
Engelhardt et al.: Sham manual acupuncture (MA) with needles inserted into points for headache.
Jiang et al.: Psychological therapy alone.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "Turkey, Austria, China" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasMappedWesternCondition MONDO:0005362 ;
    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-281 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-281 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails """Total participants: 183
Aydin et al.: 60
Engelhardt et al.: 21
Jiang et al.: 102""" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails """Engelhardt et al. (2003): Sham MA at points for headache (Xuanzhong, Jiexi, Tianshu).
Aydin et al. (1997): Sham EA at non-traditional, non-meridian points on legs and arms.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails """Electroacupuncture (EA): Needles were stimulated electrically.
Manual Acupuncture (MA): Needles were likely manipulated manually, but specific method not detailed.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper does not explicitly mention a TCM term for the condition)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Erectile dysfunction (ED)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings """One trial showed beneficial effects of acupuncture compared to sham acupuncture.
MA was significantly effective compared to sham (RR 7.53, 95% CI: 1.13-50.00, p = 0.04).
EA was not significantly superior to sham (RR 1.40, 95% CI: 0.67-2.91, p = 0.37).
Acupuncture combined with psychological therapy was significantly more effective than psychological therapy alone (RR 1.88, 95% CI: 1.38-2.55, p < 0.0001).
Overall, evidence for acupuncture alone improving ED is insufficient.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Aydin et al. [19]: Improvement in sexual activity (interview)
Engelhardt et al. [18]: Response rate (interview) erection sufficient for penetration and intercourse, IIEF-5 score
Jiang et al. [20]: Response rate (interview) erection sufficient for penetration and intercourse""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration "Not specified (the paper does not provide information on the duration of each acupuncture session or how long the needles were left in place)" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency """Aydin et al. (1997): Electroacupuncture twice a week for 6 weeks, 12 sessions.
Engelhardt et al. (2003): Manual acupuncture, frequency not specified, average of 11 sessions.
Jiang et al. (2012): Manual acupuncture once a day for 30 days.""" .

