tara-kb:S-112625-295 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-295" ;
    dc:title "Minireview Acupuncture for Chronic Nonbacterial Prostatitis/Chronic Pelvic Pain Syndrome: A Systematic Review" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol """He and Xu (2007): Sanyin points; Sanyin points plus Yinsan points, retaining needle for 30 min, 1 time per day, 30 treatments (36 days).
He et al (2004): Sanyin points (points at perineum, named by the author) and other acupoints, retaining needle for 30 min, 1 time per day, 30 treatments (36 days).
Hu et al (2005): CV3-to-CV2, BL32-to-BL34; BL54-to-BL35, retaining needle for 20 min, 1 time per day for 30 days.
Huang et al (2005): CV6, CV4, CV3, BL54, BL23, BL28, BL22, ST28, SP9, SP6, KI3, retaining needle for 20‚Äì30 min, 1 time per day. Total treatment duration: 2‚Äì4 weeks.
Jin and Ji (2008): BL54 to ST28. Other acupoints were selected individually according to the principle of traditional Chinese medicine. Retaining needle for 30 min, 1 time per day, duration 2 months.
Li and Wang (2006): CV1 and CV3. Other acupoints were selected individually according to the principle of traditional Chinese medicine. Retaining needle for 30 min, 3 times per week, total treatment duration 8 weeks.
Lee et al (2008): CV1 inserted with a depth of 50 mm, CV4 with 60 mm, and SP6 and SP9 with 40 mm. Two weekly sessions for 10 weeks, 30 min each session.
Wang (1997): CV1. Duration: 1 time per day, without retaining needle, 4 weeks (1 day stopping per week).
Zhang (2009): BL54-to-ST28, retaining needle, 30 min per treatment, tertian treating, duration 8 weeks.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Randomized Controlled Trial (RCT)" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails """Pharmacotherapy (He et al, 2004; Hu et al, 2005; Li and Wang, 2006; He and Xu, 2007; Zhang, 2009; Zhong-Xin, 2009)
Herbal medicine (Wang, 1997; Jin and Ji, 2008)
Sham acupuncture (Lee et al, 2008)""" ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "China, Malaysia" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasMappedWesternCondition MONDO:0022103 ;
    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails """CV1: 50 mm
CV4: 60 mm
SP6: 40 mm
SP9: 40 mm""" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-295 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-295 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails "890 patients" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails "Not mentioned (the paper does not provide specific details on how the sham acupuncture was performed)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails "Not specified (the paper does not provide details on how the needles were stimulated beyond their use)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper does not provide the condition in Traditional Chinese Medicine terminology)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Chronic prostatitis/chronic pelvic pain syndrome (CP/CPPS)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "Maybe/Inconclusive (the evidence is encouraging but not conclusive due to methodological flaws and lack of control for placebo effects)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measures: Effective rate (used in all Chinese-language RCTs), NIH-CPSI (used in Lee et al, 2008)
Secondary outcome measures: White blood cell level and the amount of lipophore in prostatic fluid (used in Li and Wang, 2006)""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """He and Xu (2007): Needles retained for 30 minutes, one time per day for 30 treatments.
Hu et al (2005): Needles retained for 20 minutes, one time per day for 30 days.
Huang et al (2005): Needles retained for 20‚Äì30 minutes, one time per day for 2‚Äì4 weeks.
Jin and Ji (2008): Needles retained for 30 minutes, one time per day for 2 months.
Li and Wang (2006): Needles retained for 30 minutes, three times per week for 8 weeks.
Lee et al (2008): Needles retained for 30 minutes, two weekly sessions for 10 weeks.
Wang (1997): Needles not retained, one time per day for 4 weeks.
Zhang (2009): Needles retained for 30 minutes, tertian treating, duration 8 weeks.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "Not specified in the text quotes. Detailed frequency information is likely in the tables." .

