tara-kb:S-112625-301 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-301" ;
    dc:title "Acupuncture for Frequent Migraine: A Randomized, Patient/Assessor Blinded, Controlled Trial with One-Year Follow-Up" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol """Mandatory acupoints: Fengchi (GB20), Taiyang (EX-HN5), Shuai Gu (GB8), Hegu (LI4).
Supplementary acupoints based on TCM syndromes:
Ascending hyperactivity of liver yang: Baihui (DU20), Xingjian (LR2), Taichong (LR3), Taixi (KI3), Xuanzhong (GB39), Sanyinjiao (SP6).
Deficiency of both Qi and blood: Baihui (DU20), Shang Xing (DU23), Zusanli (ST36), Sanyinjiao (SP6).
Wind phlegm blocking the meridians: Feng Long (ST40), Zhongwan (CV12), Yinlingquan (SP9).
Blood stasis: Sanyinjiao (SP6), Xuehai (SP10), Ashi point.
Sham points: Local sham points on the scalp, face, and neck; distal sham points on the four extremities.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Randomized Controlled Trial (RCT)" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails "The sham acupuncture procedure involved a combination of shallow insertion at distal points and noninsertion at cranial points. The sham points were located near real acupoints but were not the actual therapeutic points. No needle manipulation was performed to avoid De Qi sensation. The procedure aimed to enhance credibility while minimizing nonspecific effects." ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "Australia" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Yes, the acupuncture intervention was effective compared to the sham group based on the primary outcomes of reducing migraine days and severity. The statistical significance for these outcomes was reported with p-values of 0.008 and 0.004, respectively." ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupoints "GB 20, EX-HN 5, GB 8, LI 4, DU 20, LR 2, LR 3, KI 3, GB 39, SP 6, DU 23, ST 36, ST 40, CV 12, SP 9, SP 10, Ashi point" ;
