tara-kb:S-112625-304 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-304" ;
    dc:title "Effects of Long-Term Acupuncture Treatment on Resting-State Brain Activity in Migraine Patients: A Randomized Controlled Trial on Active Acupoints and Inactive Acupoints" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol """Active acupoints:
SJ5: on the dorsal aspect of the forearm on the line connecting SJ4 and the tip of the elbow, 2 cun above the transverse crease of the wrist between the ulna and radius
GB20: in a depression between the upper portion of the sternocleidomastoid muscle and the trapezius
GB34: on the lateral aspect of the lower leg in the depression anterior and inferior to the head of the fibula
GB40: anterior and inferior to the external malleolus in a depression on the lateral side of the tendon of the extensor digitorum longus
Inactive acupoints:
SJ22: on the side of the head on the posterior border of the hairline of the temple at the level with the root of the auricle, posterior to the superficial temporal artery
PC7: in the middle of the transverse crease of the wrist between the tendons of the palmaris longus and flexor carpi radialis
GB37: on the lateral aspect of the lower leg 5 cun above the tip of the external malleolus on the anterior border of the fibula
SP3: proximal and inferior to the head of the 1st metatarsal-phalangeal joint in a depression at the junction of the red and white skin""" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Randomized Controlled Trial (RCT)" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails "The control group received inactive acupoint acupuncture treatment, which was implemented on bilateral SJ22 (Erheliao), PC7 (Daling), GB37 (Guangming), and SP3 (Taibai). This served as a sham control to compare with the active acupoint group." ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "China" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupoints "SJ 5, GB 20, GB 34, GB 40, SJ 22, PC 7, GB 37, SP 3" ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupointsMapped TARA:1763744,
        TARA:2324295,
        TARA:3003448,
        TARA:6320058,
        TARA:8393005,
        TARA:8758950,
        TARA:9020892,
        TARA:9254368 ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupointsMappedCurie "{\"TARA:8393005\": \"TE 5\", \"TARA:2324295\": \"GB 20\", \"TARA:8758950\": \"GB 34\", \"TARA:9254368\": \"GB 40\", \"TARA:1763744\": \"TE 22\", \"TARA:9020892\": \"PC 7\", \"TARA:6320058\": \"GB 37\", \"TARA:3003448\": \"SP 3\"}"^^rdf:JSON ;
    tara-kb:hasMappedWesternCondition MONDO:0100431 ;
    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails "approximately 2.5 cm-3.5 cm" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-304 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-304 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails """Total sample size: 80
Active acupoint group: 40
Inactive acupoint group: 40
Participants in fMRI scans: 40 (20 in each group)""" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails """Sham procedure involved inactive acupoints: SJ22, PC7, GB37, SP3.
Same type of needles used as in active treatment: single-use stainless steel filiform needles, 25 mm-40 mm in length, 0.25 mm-0.30 mm in diameter.
Needles were penetrating.
Technique involved twisting with rotation to achieve de-qi sensation.
Performed by experienced acupuncturists.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails "Manual manipulation of needles to achieve de-qi sensation." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper does not explicitly mention a TCM term for the condition)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Migraine without aura" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings """"The neuroimaging data indicated that long-term active acupoint therapy elicited a more extensive and remarkable cerebral response compared with acupuncture at inactive acupoints."
"Correlation analysis showed that the decrease in the visual analogue scale (VAS) was significantly related to the increased average Regional homogeneity (ReHo) values in the anterior cingulate cortex in the two groups."
"Moreover, the decrease in the VAS was associated with increased average ReHo values in the insula which could be detected in the active acupoint group."
"Acupuncture at active acupoints was significantly superior to acupuncture at inactive acupoints in alleviating pain intensity (P = 0.015)."
"The difference in VAS between the active group and inactive group was more than 0.9 in week 8 (P = 0.006)."
"In the active acupoint group, the decrease in the VAS score was significantly related to the increased average ReHo values in the ACC (r = 20.6619, P,0.05) and insula (r = 20.7407, P,0.05, Bonferroni corrected)."
"In the inactive control group, the decrease in the VAS score was only significantly related to the increased average ReHo values in the ACC (r = 20.6611, P,0.05, Bonferroni corrected)."
"Based on the clinical outcomes of this RCT, both active and inactive acupuncture methods were helpful in treating migraine after 8 weeks of therapy (P,0.05)."
"Acupuncture at active acupoints might have the potential effect of regulating some disease-affected key regions and the pain circuitry for migraine."
"More importantly, our results provided some evidence that active acupuncture treatment as a holistic therapy promotes psychophysical pain homeostasis."
Yes""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measures:
Difference in resting-state brain activation patterns evoked by active acupoints and inactive acupoints via fMRI assessment
VAS score (intensity of headache)
Secondary outcome measures:
Number of days with a migraine per 4 weeks
Frequency of migraines per 4 weeks
HIT-6 questionnaire (severity and impact of headache on a patient's life)""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """Duration of each acupuncture session: 30 minutes
Duration needles were left in place: 30 minutes""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "The frequency of treatment was once every other day, preferably 4 times a week, with each session lasting 30 minutes, over an 8-week period." .

