    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Gastrointestinal symptoms (specifically: diarrhoea, loose stools, gas/bloating, abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "Yes, the acupuncture intervention was effective compared to the control/sham group(s) based on the primary outcome(s). The AR group had greater intervention effects for loose stools symptoms and significant effects on reducing nausea symptoms during the first 4 weeks of intervention. The combination of acupuncture and the relaxation response was more effective than using either modality alone." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcomes: Changes in symptom ratings for loose stools and nausea
Secondary outcomes: Effects on diarrhoea, gas/bloating, abdominal pain, and vomiting""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """Duration of each acupuncture session: 30 minutes
Duration needles were left in place: 30 minutes""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "Twice weekly for the first 4 weeks, once weekly for the following 4 weeks; each session lasted 30 minutes." .

tara-kb:S-112625-4 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-4" ;
    dc:title "Acupuncture for Acute Postoperative Pain after Back Surgery: A Systematic Review and Meta-analysis of Randomized Controlled Trials" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol """Acupoint electrical stimulation: BL40, GB34, HT7, P6
Classic acupuncture: BL25, GB31, BL26, GB30, BL62, BL23, BL36, BL40, GB34
Abdominal acupuncture: CV12, CV9, CV6, CV4; individualized points based on surgery location
Auricular acupressure: TF4, AT3, AH9, CO4, CO18""" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Randomized Controlled Trial (RCT)" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails """Sham/placebo acupuncture: nonpenetrating on acupoints or superficial penetrating on nonacupoints
No treatment
Conventional therapy: drugs, rehabilitation""" ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "Taiwan, Germany, China" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasMappedWesternCondition HP:0012531 ;
    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-4 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-4 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails """Yeh (2011): 90 participants
Yeh (2010a): 94 participants
Wang (2000): 132 participants
Li (2008): 90 participants
Yeh (2010b): 74 participants""" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails "The sham acupuncture procedures involved nonpenetrating on acupoints or superficial penetrating on nonacupoints. Specific details about needle type, quantity, and size are not provided. In some studies, sham acupoint electrical stimulation was used." ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails """Acupoint electrical stimulation with nonpenetration (Studies 34, 38)
Classic acupuncture with manual stimulation (Study 36)
Abdominal acupuncture with classic acupuncture (Study 35)
Acupressure on the ear (Study 37)
Other methods mentioned include lasers, ultrasound, and electricity, but specific use in studies is not detailed.
Type of sensation sought: Not specified""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper does not explicitly translate the conditions into Traditional Chinese Medicine terminology)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Acute postoperative pain after back surgery due to intervertebral disk herniation, spinal stenosis, or spondylolisthesis" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings """The meta-analysis showed significant improvements in pain intensity as measured by VAS 24 hours after surgery when comparing acupuncture to sham acupuncture (standard mean difference -0.67, p = 0.0003).
There was no significant improvement in postoperative opiate demands when comparing acupuncture to sham acupuncture (standard mean difference -0.23, p = 0.21).
The review concludes that there is encouraging but limited evidence for the effectiveness of acupuncture in reducing acute postoperative pain.
Acupuncture was effective compared to the sham group in terms of pain intensity but not in terms of opiate demands.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcomes: Visual analogue scale (VAS) for pain intensity, opiate demands
Secondary outcomes: Adverse events of acupuncture""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration "Not specified (the paper does not provide information on the duration of each acupuncture session or how long the needles were left in place)" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "Not specified (the paper does not provide explicit information on the frequency or duration of acupuncture treatments)" .

