tara-kb:S-112625-433 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-433" ;
    dc:title "Acupuncture for primary dysmenorrhoea: a systematic review" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol """SP6 acupoint was commonly selected in 17 trials.
Various acupoints for acupuncture treatments were used in the included RCTs.
Types of acupuncture included body acupuncture, warm acupuncture, auricular acupuncture, auricular acupressure, electroacupuncture, electrical auricular acupuncture, and acupoint injection.
Both traditional (classical meridian points) and contemporary (nonmeridian or trigger points) acupuncture methods were used.
Specific details about acupoints used in each study are likely found in the tables at the end of the paper, but not specified here.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Randomized Controlled Trial (RCT)" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails """No treatment (wait-listed or treatment as usual)
Placebo-controlled (sham acupuncture, minimal acupuncture)
Pharmacological treatment (nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs or oral contraceptive pill)
Nonpharmacological interventions (psychological interventions)""" ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "China, Korea, Canada" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-433 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-433 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails "Total sample size: 2960 subjects; Sample size within groups: Not specified (individual study sample sizes are provided in the tables)" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails "Sham acupuncture was used in some studies. Study 18 used sham acupuncture without the 'degi' effect at points on the thighs and arms not on classically described meridians. Study 16 used sham acupuncture at non-acupoints. Study 17 used minimal acupuncture at points unrelated to dysmenorrhoea treatment. Specific details about needle type, quantity, and size are not provided." ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails """Electroacupuncture: Electrical stimulation
Electrical auricular acupuncture: Electrical stimulation
Warm acupuncture: Thermal stimulation
Auricular acupressure: Pressure stimulation
Acupoint injection: Injection stimulation
Other types of stimulation (e.g., manual manipulation, laser) are not specified.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Primary dysmenorrhoea" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings """Acupuncture is effective compared to pharmacological treatment or herbal medicine: Yes
Acupuncture is effective compared to sham acupuncture: No""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcomes: Pain relief for dichotomous data (number of women with pain relief, reduced pain or no improvement), improvement in symptoms for continuous data (measured by visual analogue scale or other scales)
Secondary outcomes: Adverse effects from treatment (incidence and type of side-effect)""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration "Not specified (the paper does not provide information on the duration of each acupuncture session or the needle retention time)" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency """General: Acupuncture interventions were usually introduced 3-7 days prior to the onset of menstruation and continued for approximately 7 days.
Duration: The periods of treatment ranged from 1 day to three menstrual cycles.
Specific frequencies and durations are detailed in the tables at the end of the paper.""" .

