tara-kb:S-112625-436 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-436" ;
    dc:title "Immediate Analgesia Effect of Single Point Acupuncture in Primary Dysmenorrhea: A Randomized Controlled Trial" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol """Sanyinjiao (SP6): Junction point of spleen, liver, and kidney meridians; related to lower abdomen and uterus.
Xuanzhong (GB39): Point of the gall bladder meridian; typically used for stiff neck, headache, back pain, and shoulder pain.
Nonacupoint location: Lateral side of lower leg, 3 inches above the tip of external malleolus, 1.5 inches behind anterior crest of the tibia.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Randomized Controlled Trial (RCT)" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails """Acupoint group: Received acupuncture at Sanyinjiao (SP6).
Unrelated acupoint group: Received acupuncture at Xuanzhong (GB39), serving as a sham acupuncture control.
Nonacupoint group: Received needle insertion at a non acupuncture point, serving as another sham acupuncture control.
No acupuncture group: Did not receive any acupuncture treatment, serving as a control group.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "China" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupoints "SP 6, GB 39" ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupointsMapped TARA:3770332,
        TARA:4385250 ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupointsMappedCurie "{\"TARA:4385250\": \"SP 6\", \"TARA:3770332\": \"GB 39\"}"^^rdf:JSON ;
    tara-kb:hasMappedWesternCondition MONDO:1060206 ;
    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails "Not specified (the paper provides needle lengths but does not explicitly state the depth of insertion)" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-436 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-436 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails """Total participants analyzed: 194
Acupoint group: 49
Unrelated acupoint group: 49
Nonacupoint group: 48
No acupuncture group: 48""" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails """Sham procedure 1: Penetrating sham using an unrelated acupoint (Xuanzhong, GB39) with real needles (0.25 mm ¬• 40 mm).
Sham procedure 2: Penetrating sham using a nonacupoint location (lateral side of lower leg) with real needles (0.25 mm ¬• 40 mm).""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails "The stimulation used in the study was electro acupuncture with bipolar stimulating electrodes in an alternating frequency mode (2/100 Hz) for 30 minutes. The goal was to achieve \"De Qi,\" a radiating feeling indicative of effective needling." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper does not explicitly state the TCM terminology for primary dysmenorrhea)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Primary dysmenorrhea" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings """The primary comparison of VAS scores demonstrated that patients receiving acupuncture had a significant reduction in pain intensity ( 15.56 mm, 95% CI22.16 to8.95, P < 0.001).
Acupuncture was effective in reducing pain compared to no acupuncture but not significantly different from sham acupuncture.
The specificity of the immediate pain alleviating effect in classic acupoints needs further investigation.
Yes, acupuncture was effective compared to the control group (no acupuncture), but not significantly different from sham acupuncture.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measure:Pain intensity as measured by a 100 mm visual analogScale (VAS) at baseline; 5, 10, 30, and 60 minutes following theStart of the first intervention.
Secondary outcome measures: Cox retrospectiveSymptomScale (RSS), verbal ratingScale (VRS),Pain total time, andProportion ofParticipants using analgesics.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """Duration of each acupuncture session: 30 minutes
Duration needles were left in place: Not specified""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "Acupuncture and sham acupuncture were administered once daily for 3 days, with each session lasting 30 minutes." .

