tara-kb:S-112625-442 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-442" ;
    dc:title "Acupuncture for premenstrual syndrome: a systematic review and meta-analysis of randomised controlled trials" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol """Points were commonly used in general acupuncture practice for obstetrics/gynaecology conditions.
Additional points were used based on symptoms.
Selection was based on traditional Chinese medicine (TCM) style.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails """Sham acupuncture: Three trials used sham acupuncture as a control. Two trials used superficial needling at non-acupuncture points, and one trial used identical needling at real but irrelevant acupuncture points.
Medication: Four trials compared acupuncture with medication. Medications included progestin (medroxyprogesterone) alone or in combination with diazepam.
No treatment: One Korean study compared acupuncture with no treatment.
Herbal medicine: One trial compared acupuncture combined with herbal medicine to herbal medicine alone.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "China, Korea, Croatia" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasMappedWesternCondition MONDO:0004169 ;
    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails "Not specified (the paper does not provide a comprehensive or standardized description of needle insertion depth across all studies)" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-442 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-442 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails """Total sample size: Not specified
Acupuncture versus all controls: 429 women
Acupuncture versus medication: 232 women
Acupuncture versus sham acupuncture: 108 women
Acupuncture versus no treatment: 14 women
Acupuncture and medication versus medication alone: 42 women
Acupuncture and herbal medicine versus herbal medicine alone: 60 women""" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails """One study used identical needling on real acupuncture points considered ineffective for PMS.
Two studies used superficial needling at non-acupuncture points.
Yu (2006): Superficial acupuncture at non-acupuncture points 1 cm away from basic points at 0.2‚Äì0.3 cun depth, unclear de-qi response.
Kim (2005): Acupuncture at SI5 and ST40 considered ineffective for PMS, de-qi elicited.
Habek (2002): Superficial acupuncture at non-acupuncture points without de-qi.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails "Manual stimulation, such as twirling or lifting/thrusting the needle to elicit the de qi sensation (soreness, numbness, distension, heaviness, or electric shock sensation)." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper does not provide a specific TCM disease or condition terminology for PMS)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Premenstrual syndrome (PMS)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "Yes, acupuncture was found to be effective compared to control/sham groups based on the primary outcomes, with statistically significant results (p-values < 0.00001). However, the evidence is weakened by methodological flaws in the studies." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measures: Mean differences for continuous data, risk ratios for dichotomous data, Menstrual Symptom Severity List (MSSL), clinical improvement based on a four-point Likert-type scale.
Secondary outcome measures: Not specified.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration "Not specified (the paper does not provide explicit information on the duration of each acupuncture session or how long the needles were left in place)" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "Not specified (the paper does not provide explicit information on the frequency of treatment, such as how often acupuncture was administered per week)" .

