    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-49 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails """Total sample size: 60
Sample size per group: 30 (Manual Acupuncture), 30 (Electroacupuncture)""" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails "Not applicable (no sham acupuncture group was used in the study)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails """Electroacupuncture (EA): Electrical stimulation with frequencies of 3 Hz and 100 Hz, pulse durations of 1 ms and 0.88 ms, respectively, for 30 minutes. The intensity was adjusted to a strong but comfortable level.
Manual Acupuncture (MA): Manual manipulation of needles every 3 minutes for a total of 30 minutes.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper does not mention the condition in Traditional Chinese Medicine terminology)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Knee osteoarthritis" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings """Results: Both manual acupuncture and electroacupuncture showed significant reductions in pain intensity (p<0.001) and TUG test time (p=0.005 for MA, p=0.002 for EA). No significant differences were found between the groups for any outcomes (p=0.25 for pain intensity, p=0.70 for TUG test).
Conclusion: No difference between the immediate effects of MA and EA on pain, muscle strength, and mobility in patients with knee OA.
Effectiveness: Yes (both interventions were effective in reducing pain and improving mobility, but not different from each other).""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measure: Pain intensity (pain NRS 0-10)
Secondary outcome measures: Degree of dysfunction (Timed Up and Go (TUG) test), maximal voluntary isometric contraction, pressure pain threshold""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration "30 minutes for both MA and EA sessions; needles were left in place for the entire duration of each session." ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "A single session of treatment was administered. The frequency of treatment was once." .

