    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails """Body Acupuncture (BA): Needles were stimulated manually by rotating them clockwise and counterclockwise for 15 seconds each, with six rotations per second.
Tongue Acupuncture (TA): Needles were inserted obliquely and then immediately removed.
Sensation sought: De qi sensation, characterized by feelings of distension, heaviness, and numbness.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper does not provide a specific TCM term for depression)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Depression" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "Yes, the acupuncture intervention was effective compared to the control/sham group(s) based on the primary outcome(s). The study found that both BA and BA + TA improved clinical scores and HRV, with TA enhancing the effects of BA. The p-values for the HAM-A score were 0.008 for BA and <0.001 for BA + TA, indicating significant improvements." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measures: Heart rate (HR), Heart rate variability (HRV), Hamilton Anxiety Rating Scale (HAM-A), Athens Insomnia Scale (AIS), Hamilton Rating Scale for Depression (HRSD)
