tara-kb:S-112625-556 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-556" ;
    dc:title "Systematic Review of Acupuncture to Control Hot Flashes in Cancer Patients" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol """General: 29 points involving 11 channels (all major channels except Small Intestine), Governing Vessel, and Conception Vessel.
Common points: Spleen 6, Liver 3, Kidney 3, Stomach 36.
Specific points per study:
Bao 2014: CV4, CV6, CV12, bilateral LI4, SJ6, Gb34, St36, K3, and B65.
Bokmand & Flyger 2013: bilateral H6, K3, Sp6, and Lv3.
Liljegren 2012: LI4, H6, Lv3, and St36 unilaterally and Sp6 and K7 bilaterally.
Frisk 2008: EA bilaterally from B23 to B32 + GV20 and bilateral B15, H7, Sp6, Sp9, Lv3, and P6.
Hervik & Mjaland 2009: Lv3, Gb20, Lu7, K3, Sp6, Cv4, P7, and Lv8.
Walker 2010: Bilateral core points: B23, K3, and Sp6. Secondary points based on TCM diagnosis: GV14, GV20, Gb20, Lu9, Lv3, St36, CV6, P7, and H7.
Nedstrand 2006: EA bilaterally from B23 to B32 + GV20 and bilateral B15, H7, Sp6, Sp9, Lv3, and P6.
Deng 2007: GV14, bilateral Gb20, B13, P7, H6, K7, St36, and Sp6 + ear Shenmen and ear Sympathetic.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Systematic Review" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails """Sham acupuncture (SA)
Applied relaxation
Pharmacological therapy (e.g., venlafaxine, hormone replacement therapy)
No acupuncture""" ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "Not specified (the paper is a systematic review and does not specify the country or countries where the included studies were conducted)" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasMappedWesternCondition HP:0031217 ;
    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-556 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-556 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails "Total participants: 474; Specific sample sizes for each study are detailed in the tables at the end of the paper." ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails """Sham acupuncture involved non-penetrating devices or superficial needling.
Locations were non-acupuncture points, varying by study.
Non-penetrating devices were used in some studies.
Superficial needling was used at points not on traditional meridians.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails "Manual stimulation was used in six studies, and electrostimulation (electroacupuncture) was used in three studies. No other forms of stimulation were mentioned." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Hot flashes (HFs)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "Maybe/Inconclusive (the paper reports some significant between-group differences in favor of true acupuncture over sham acupuncture, but concludes that the overall evidence is insufficient to definitively support or refute the benefits of acupuncture for managing hot flashes in cancer patients)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure "Primary outcomes: Number of hot flashes per 24 hours, number of hot flashes per day and night. Secondary outcomes: Not specified." ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration "Not specified (the paper does not provide explicit information on the duration of each acupuncture session or how long the needles were left in place)" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "The most common frequency of treatment sessions was twice weekly, with an average of 12 treatments per trial (range, 5-16; median, 14). Follow-up periods ranged from 0 to 24 months." .

