tara-kb:S-112625-568 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-568" ;
    dc:title "Effects of Acupuncture and Acupressure on Cancer-Related Fatigue: A Systematic Review" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol "ST36, SP6, KI3; Not specified (locations on body or selection criteria)" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Systematic Review" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails "The control groups used in the studies reviewed include sham acupuncture (Balk et al., 2009; Deng et al., 2013; Smith et al., 2013), sham acupressure (Molassiotis et al., 2007), routine nursing care (Xu et al., 2010), usual discharge advice (Liu et al., 2013), usual care from physicians (Johnston et al., 2011), and weekly nurse-led supportive care (Lim et al., 2011)." ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "United States, United Kingdom, China, Australia, Canada" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasMappedWesternCondition HP:0012378 ;
    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-568 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-568 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails """Total participants: 731
Participants per study: 13-302
Specific sample sizes for each study or group: Not specified""" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails """Smith et al. (2013): Sham acupuncture group using sham acupoints with sham needles.
Deng et al. (2013): Sham acupuncture using sham acupoints with sham needles.
Balk et al. (2009): Sham acupuncture with the same frequency and duration as true acupuncture.
Molassiotis et al. (2007): Sham acupressure group taught to perform self-acupressure on non-energy-related acupoints.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails "Not specified (the paper does not explicitly state the type of stimulation used for acupuncture)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper does not explicitly translate cancer-related fatigue into Traditional Chinese Medicine terminology)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Cancer-related fatigue (CRF)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "Yes, acupuncture was found to be effective compared to control/sham groups in some studies, with statistically significant improvements reported in several cases. However, the overall conclusion is that while acupuncture tends to be effective, the results are inconclusive due to methodologic flaws and variability in study designs." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measures: Brief Fatigue Inventory (BFI), Multidimensional Fatigue Inventory (MFI), Edmonton Symptom Assessment System (ESAS), Piper Fatigue Scale (PFS), Functional Assessment of Chronic Illness Therapy-Fatigue Subscale (FACIT-F), Cancer-Related Fatigue Distress Scale (CRFDS)
Secondary outcome measures: Mental fatigue, activity, motivation, anxiety, depression, quality of life (QOL)""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """Duration of each acupuncture session: 20-30 minutes
Duration of needles left in place during a session: Not specified""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency """Acupuncture: Weekly regimen for most studies, with sessions lasting about 20-30 minutes.
Acupressure: Frequency varied from three times a week to once daily; some studies used daily acupressure.
Duration: Acupuncture ranged from 2 to 10 weeks; acupressure ranged from 10 days to 12 weeks.""" .

