    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails """T/S and N/S groups: Manual stimulation by lifting and thrusting while rotating the needle (12 times at 180 degrees clockwise and counterclockwise). Characteristic de qi sensations were sought in the T/S group.
T/0 and N/0 groups: No stimulation.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper does not explicitly translate fibromyalgia into a specific TCM disease or condition term)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Fibromyalgia" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings """"Overall pain improvement was noted with 25%-35% of subjects having a clinically significant decrease in pain; however this was not dependent upon 'correct' needle stimulation (t œ≠ 1.03; p œ≠ 0.307) or location (t œ≠ 0.76; p œ≠ 0.450)."
"An overall dose effect of treatment was observed, with three sessions weekly providing more analgesia than sessions once weekly (t œ≠ 2.10; p œ≠ 0.039)."
"Among treatment responders, improvements in pain, fatigue, and physical function were highly codependent (all p ’Ö 0.002)."
"Although needle insertion led to analgesia and improvement in other somatic symptoms, correct needle location and stimulation were not crucial."
"The present data provide evidence that a subset of individuals obtained improvements in clinical pain, fatigue, and function from acupuncture, although efficacy was not related to needle placement or stimulation."
"In this controlled trial, a subset of FM subjects received symptom improvement with acupuncture needle insertion, but no specific effects of needle placement or stimulation were noted."
The acupuncture intervention was effective in improving symptoms, but not specifically due to traditional needle placement or stimulation.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measure: Pain assessed by a numerical rating scale
Secondary outcome measures: Fatigue assessed by the Multi-dimensional Fatigue Inventory, Physical function assessed by the Short Form-36""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """Duration of each acupuncture session: Not specified
Needles left in place: 20 minutes""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency """Initial treatment frequency: once weekly
Second phase: twice weekly
Final phase: three times weekly
Total number of treatments: 18
Duration of each phase: 3 weeks
Washout period between phases: 2 weeks""" .

tara-kb:S-112625-63 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-63" ;
    dc:title "Acupuncture and Knee Osteoarthritis A Three-Armed Randomized Trial" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol """Traditional Chinese Acupuncture (TCA) points:
Obligatory points based on traditional Chinese theory for treating knee pain (Bi syndrome).
Additional points chosen based on traditional Chinese diagnosis (meridian theory and syndrome differentiation).
Ahshi points.
Sham Acupuncture points:
Minimal-depth needling without stimulation at 10 points at defined distances from TCA points.
Specific locations:
Between the gallbladder and stomach meridian on the distal part of the fibula, 2 cun above the malleolus lateralis toward the knee.
Two points 2 cun and 6 cun above the malleolus medialis in the center of the tibia surface area.
One point in the center of the thigh on the connecting line from the center of the patella to the anterior superior iliac spine.
One point on the highest spot of the tightened musculus biceps brachii.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Randomized Controlled Trial (RCT)" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails """Conservative Therapy: This group received standard medical treatment with up to 6 physiotherapy sessions and as-needed anti-inflammatory drugs, but no acupuncture.
Sham Acupuncture: This group received minimal-depth needling without stimulation at non-acupuncture points, serving as a control to assess the specific effects of TCA.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "Germany" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupoints "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasMappedWesternCondition HP:0005086 ;
    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails """Traditional Chinese Acupuncture: 0.5‚Äì3.5 cm until deqi
Sham Acupuncture: Up to 0.5 cm without deqi""" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-63 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-63 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails """Total participants analyzed: 1007
Participants within groups:
Traditional Chinese Acupuncture (TCA): 326
Sham Acupuncture: 365
Conservative Therapy: 316
Per-protocol analysis set:
TCA: 200
Sham Acupuncture: 223
Conservative Therapy: 188""" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails """Sham acupuncture involved minimal-depth needling without stimulation.
Needles were inserted at 10 points at defined distances from traditional Chinese acupuncture (TCA) points.
Specific locations included points on the fibula, tibia, thigh, and musculus biceps brachii.
The same type of needles was used as in TCA, but with minimal depth and no stimulation.
Noninvasive sham devices were not used.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails """Traditional Chinese Acupuncture (TCA): Needles were stimulated twice, but the method of stimulation is not specified.
Sham Acupuncture: No stimulation was applied.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Bi syndrome (knee pain)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Osteoarthritis of the knee" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings """"Success rates were 53.1% for TCA, 51.0% for sham acupuncture, and 29.1% for conservative therapy."
"Acupuncture groups had higher success rates than conservative therapy groups (relative risk for TCA compared with conservative therapy, 1.75 [95% CI, 1.43 to 2.13]; relative risk for sham acupuncture compared with conservative therapy, 1.73 [CI, 1.42 to 2.11])."
"There was no difference between TCA and sham acupuncture (relative risk, 1.01 [CI, 0.87 to 1.17])."
"Compared with physiotherapy and as-needed antiinflammatory drugs, addition of either TCA or sham acupuncture led to greater improvement in WOMAC score at 26 weeks."
"No statistically significant difference was observed between TCA and sham acupuncture, suggesting that the observed differences could be due to placebo effects, differences in intensity of provider contact, or a physiologic effect of needling regardless of whether it is done according to TCA principles."
"The observed success rates were 53.1% for the TCA group, 51.0% for the sham acupuncture group, and 29.1% for the conservative therapy group."
"Random-effects multivariate logistic regression analysis confirmed the existence of strong overall differences among treatment groups (P < 0.001 for global comparison)."
"Pair-wise comparisons showed statistically significantly increased success rates in the TCA and sham acupuncture groups compared with the conservative therapy group (P < 0.001 for both comparisons) and no difference between the TCA and sham acupuncture groups (P = 0.48)."
"The relative risks and the absolute risk differences, respectively, for the unadjusted success rates were 1.75 (95% CI, 1.43 to 2.13) and 24.0% (CI, 16.6% to 31.3%) for TCA versus conservative therapy, 1.73 (CI, 1.42 to 2.11) and 21.8% (CI, 14.7% to 29.0%) for sham acupuncture versus conservative therapy, and 1.01 (CI, 0.87 to 1.17) and 2.1% (CI, ‚àí5.4% to 9.6%) for TCA versus sham acupuncture."
Yes, the acupuncture intervention was effective compared to the control/sham group(s) based on the primary outcome(s).""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measure: Success rate defined by at least 36% improvement in Western Ontario and McMaster Universities Osteoarthritis Index (WOMAC) score at 26 weeks.
Secondary outcome measures: Total WOMAC score, global patient assessment, SF-12 physical subscale, SF-12 mental subscale.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """Duration of each acupuncture session: 20‚Äì30 minutes
Duration needles were left in place: Not specified""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency """Frequency: Approximately 1.67 sessions per week over 6 weeks.
Total sessions: 10 sessions initially, with up to 5 additional sessions possible.
Duration: Sessions lasted 20‚Äì30 minutes.""" .

