    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings """Acupuncture had no significant effect on the frequency and severity of hot flush (p = 0.34; p = 0.33).
Acupuncture significantly ameliorated menopause symptoms (p = 0.009).
Acupuncture is non-inferior to hormone therapy or other applied relaxation therapies in alleviating menopause symptoms.
Yes, acupuncture was effective compared to control/sham groups for menopause symptoms, but not for hot flush.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcomes: Frequency and severity of hot flush, menopause symptoms (measured by the Kupperman index)
Secondary outcomes: Not specified""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """Duration of each session: 15 to 30 minutes
Frequency: Once to twice a week
Duration needles were left in place: Not specified""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency """Lesi (2016): Once per week for 10 sessions
Mao (2015): Twice per week for 2 weeks, then once per week for 6 weeks
Bao (2014): Weekly
Hervik (2014): 15 treatments
Bokmand (2013): Weekly for 15-20 minutes
Liljegren (2012): Twice a week for 5 weeks
Walker (2010): Twice-weekly for 4 weeks, then weekly for 8 weeks
Hervik (2009): Twice-weekly for 5 weeks, then weekly for 5 weeks
Frisk (2008): Twice-weekly for 2 weeks, then weekly for 10 weeks
Deng (2007): Twice-weekly for 4 weeks
Nedstrand (2006 and 2005): Twice a week for 2 weeks, then weekly for 10 weeks""" .

tara-kb:S-112625-682 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-682" ;
    dc:title "Effectiveness and safety of acupuncture in the treatment of Parkinson's disease: A systematic review and meta-analysis of randomized controlled trials" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol """Scalp and body acupuncture were commonly used.
Specific acupoints mentioned include:GB20, LI11, GB34, ST36, LR3, GV20, PC6, GV26, LI4, SP6, GV14, BL10, GB12, SI3, BL62, PD6, Bafeng points.
Special methods included Korean traditional Sasang-Constitution acupuncture, bee venom acupuncture, Fangshi scalp acupuncture, and pharmacopuncture.
Selection criteria for acupoints not specified.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis of Randomized Controlled Trials (RCTs)" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails """Conventional medication (CM) alone: Used in 35 studies as a control group.
Non-acupoints: Used as a comparator in some studies to mimic sham acupuncture.
No treatment/waitlist: Four studies did not provide any intervention in the control group.
Placebo acupuncture: Used in some studies to compare with actual acupuncture.""" ;
