tara-kb:S-112625-7 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-7" ;
    dc:title "Postoperative Pain: Acupuncture versus Percutaneous Electrical Nerve Stimulation" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol """Sp6: Located at the posterior margin of the tibia, 3 units directly above the medial malleolus of the ankle.
Sp8: Located at the posterior margin of the tibia, 3 units below the inferior margin of the medial condyle of the tibia.
Chosen for lower gynecologic and skin disorders, as well as for enhancing energy levels.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Randomized Controlled Trial (RCT)" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails "Not applicable (the study compares two intervention groups without a separate control group)" ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "United States" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupoints "SP 6, SP 8" ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupointsMapped TARA:4385250,
        TARA:6848518 ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupointsMappedCurie "{\"TARA:4385250\": \"SP 6\", \"TARA:6848518\": \"SP 8\"}"^^rdf:JSON ;
    tara-kb:hasMappedWesternCondition HP:0012531 ;
    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-7 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-7 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails "Total: 18; Traditional Acupuncture: 9; Acupuncture with PENS: 9" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails "Not applicable (no sham group was used in the study)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails """Traditional Acupuncture: Manual stimulation of needles twice daily.
Acupuncture with PENS: Electrical stimulation using embedded portable electrodes in modulation mode, adjusted to ensure patient sensation.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Postoperative pain in gynecologic oncology patients" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings """The PENS group demonstrated a consistent decrease in pain with each treatment application compared to traditional acupuncture.
After 48 hours, both groups experienced equivalent pain relief.
PENS was more effective than traditional acupuncture in reducing postoperative pain.
The total MPQ pain scores showed a 14.6% decrease for PENS and a 1.9% decrease for traditional acupuncture.
The study does not provide p-values or statistical significance for the primary outcomes.
Yes, the acupuncture intervention with PENS was effective compared to traditional acupuncture based on the primary outcome.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measure: Reduction in postoperative pain assessed using the visual analog scale (VAS) and the McGill Pain Questionnaire (MPQ).
Secondary outcome measures: Ambulation and opiate consumption (not significantly different between groups).""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """Duration of each acupuncture session: 45-60 minutes
Duration needles were left in place: Until the end of each day (after the second treatment of the day)""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "Four treatments within 48 hours, with 12 hours between each treatment. Treatments were initiated within 24 hours after surgery." .

