    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails "2.5-3.5 cm" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-705 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-705 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails """Total sample size: 38
Experiment 1: 8
Experiment 2: 10
Experiment 3: 5
Experiment 4: 5""" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails """Sham acupuncture was performed by inserting needles under the skin at a point one third of the way between the elbow and wrist on the lateral aspect of the nondominant forearm, which is not a known acupoint.
The depth of insertion was 2.5-3.5 cm, similar to the real electroacupuncture.
Electrical stimulation was performed in an identical manner to electroacupuncture.
The sham procedure involved actual penetration of the skin.
The subjects reported similar sensations during the sham procedure as during the real electroacupuncture.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails "The stimulation used in the study was electrical stimulation (electroacupuncture) with a continuous wave, biphasic pulse of 8 Hz. The intensity was gradually increased to the maximum tolerated without discomfort or fasciculation. The sensation sought was the \"qi\" or needling reaction." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper does not explicitly mention a specific TCM disease or condition)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Not specified (the study focuses on physiological responses in healthy individuals rather than a specific disease or condition)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "Yes, the acupuncture intervention was effective compared to the control/sham group(s) based on the primary outcome(s) of reducing basal and sham feeding-stimulated acid output, with statistical significance (P < 0.05 and P < 0.005, respectively)." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measures:
Effect of electroacupuncture on basal acid output
Effect of electroacupuncture on sham feeding-stimulated acid output
Secondary outcome measures:
Effect of electroacupuncture on pentagastrin-stimulated acid output
Influence of local anesthesia on the acupoint
Effect of intravenous naloxone on sham feeding-stimulated acid output
Effect of acupuncture on serum gastrin levels""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration "Duration of each acupuncture session: 15 minutes; Duration needles were left in place: Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "Not specified (the paper does not provide information on the frequency of treatment sessions beyond the experimental design)" .

