    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "- Back pain - Knee pain - Headache - Fibromyalgia - Post-stroke rehabilitation - Oocyte collection" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings """Acupuncture was superior to usual care for back pain, knee pain, and headache.
Acupuncture was superior to placebo for chronic knee pain, but not for fibromyalgia or post-stroke rehabilitation.
Acupuncture was not as effective as conventional analgesia for oocyte collection.
Effectiveness: Yes for chronic knee pain and headache; No for fibromyalgia and post-stroke rehabilitation; Not as effective as conventional analgesia for oocyte collection.
Statistical significance (p-values): Not specified.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measures: Motor rating scale, Parkinson Disease Questionnaire, Barthel ADL, Pain VAS
Secondary outcome measures: Geriatric depression scale, Grip, ROM, ADL, QOL, Mood, Stroke infarction assessment scale, Ashworth, isodynamic test of spasticity""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration "Not specified (the paper does not consistently provide the duration of each acupuncture session or how long the needles were left in place)" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "Many studies, especially those with large sample sizes, gave acupuncture twice weekly. Specific frequencies and durations are detailed in the tables for various conditions." .

