    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails "The sham intervention used superficial needle insertion at body locations not recognized as true acupoints. The procedure involved actual needle insertion but at non-traditional points to control for acupoint specificity. Details on needle type, quantity, and size are not provided." ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails "Manual manipulation was used to stimulate the needles, with the goal of obtaining and re-elicitating a de qi sensation." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper does not explicitly translate the condition into Traditional Chinese Medicine terminology)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Breast cancer with AI-induced arthralgias" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "Yes, the acupuncture intervention was effective compared to the sham control group based on the primary outcomes. The primary end point showed a significant difference in mean BPI-SF worst pain scores at 6 weeks (P < .001), with additional significant improvements in pain severity and pain-related interference. The study concludes that acupuncture is an effective and well-tolerated strategy for managing AI-induced arthralgias." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary Outcome Measure: Difference in mean BPI-SF worst pain scores at 6 weeks.
Secondary Outcome Measures: Western Ontario and McMaster Universities Osteoarthritis Index (WOMAC)
Modified Score for the Assessment of Chronic Rheumatoid Affections of the Hands (M-SACRAH)
Physical well-being measured by the FACT-G
Change in analgesic use
Frequency and severity of adverse events according to the National Cancer Institute Common Terminology Criteria version 3.0""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """Duration of each acupuncture session: 30 minutes
Duration needles were left in place: 20 to 25 minutes""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "Twice weekly for 6 weeks, with each session lasting 30 minutes. A total of 12 sessions were administered." .

