tara-kb:S-112625-74 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-74" ;
    dc:title "Efficacy of Periosteal Stimulation Therapy for the Treatment of Osteoarthritis-Associated Chronic Knee Pain: An Initial Controlled Clinical Trial" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol """Medial femoral condyle
Lateral femoral condyle
Flare of tibia
Head of fibula
Soft tissue on either side of the upper third of the tibial shaft""" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Randomized Controlled Trial (RCT)" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails "The control group received control PST, which involved the insertion of needles without the active electrical stimulation of the periosteum. Additional needles were inserted and stimulated for a short period to control for treatment credibility." ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "United States" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupoints "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasMappedWesternCondition HP:0005086 ;
    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-74 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-74 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails """Total sample size: 88
Participants within groups: Not specified (though implied to be roughly equal due to randomization)""" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails """Needle type: Real needles were used in both groups.
Quantity: Four needles were used for periosteal stimulation in the PST group; two additional needles were used in both groups for control.
Size: 30-gauge needles were used.
Location: Needles were inserted into bony prominences for PST; into soft tissue for control.
Penetration: Needles were penetrating in both groups.
Stimulation: PST group received 100 Hz stimulation for 30 minutes; control group received 100 Hz stimulation for 1 minute.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails "The stimulation in Debra K Weiner, Thomas E Rudy, Natalia Morone, Ronald Glick, C Kent Kwoh is electrical stimulation (electroacupuncture) at a frequency of 100 Hz for 30 minutes. The intensity was adjusted to be clearly felt but not uncomfortable." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper does not mention any Traditional Chinese Medicine terminology or conditions)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Osteoarthritis (OA) of the knee" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "Yes, the acupuncture intervention (PST) was effective compared to the control group in reducing pain at post-intervention and 1 month later, as indicated by significant p-values (P 5.003 and Po.001). However, pain levels regressed by 2 months, and there were no significant differences in physical performance or analgesic use." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary Outcome Measures:
Western Ontario and McMaster Universities Osteoarthritis Index (WOMAC) for pain and self-reported function
Short Physical Performance Battery (SPPB) for mobility and disability risk
Multidimensional Pain Inventory (MPI) short form for pain severity
Secondary Outcome Measures:
Geriatric Depression Scale for mood
Pittsburgh Sleep Quality Index for sleep hygiene
Frequency of as-needed analgesic ingestion
Timed stair climbing task
Self-rated health""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """Duration of each acupuncture session: 30 minutes
Needles left in place during a session: For the entire 30 minutes
Frequency of treatment: Once a week for 6 weeks""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "Once a week for 6 weeks, with each session lasting 30 minutes." .

