    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Postoperative pain after ambulatory knee surgery" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "Yes, the acupuncture intervention was effective compared to the control/sham group based on the primary outcome of reduced ibuprofen requirement (p = 0.012)." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary Outcome Measure: Postoperative requirement for ibuprofen between surgery and examination the following morning.
Secondary Outcome Measures: Heart rate and blood pressure, incidence of analgesia-related side effects, patient opinions on blinding success, ibuprofen requirement, patients needing tramadol, piritramide dose, discharge time, follow-up time, night sleep duration, number of night arousals, incidence of adverse events related to analgesia and acupuncture.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """Duration of needle retention: Overnight (from before surgery until the following morning)
Duration of each acupuncture session: Not specified""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "The acupuncture treatment was administered once, with needles inserted before surgery and retained until the following morning. Patients were encouraged to stimulate the needles as needed. No specific frequency of treatment beyond this is mentioned." .

