    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails "The stimulation involved the insertion of Aiguille Semi-Permanent (ASP) needles bilaterally at two acupuncture points on the ear using a rapid insertion method to decrease discomfort. No additional stimulation techniques such as manual manipulation, electrical stimulation, or thermal stimulation were mentioned. The needles were left in place for approximately 4 to 6 days without further stimulation." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper does not provide TCM terminology for the condition being studied)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Acute pain syndromes" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "Yes, the acupuncture intervention was effective compared to the control group based on the primary outcome of immediate pain reduction in the ER (p < 0.0005). However, the long-term effect at 24 hours was not significantly different between groups." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary Outcome Measure: Change in pain level from baseline, as measured by the Numerical Rating Scale (NRS).
Secondary Outcome Measures: Not specified.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """Duration of each acupuncture session: Not specified
Duration needles were left in place: Approximately 4 to 6 days""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "Not specified (the paper does not provide information on the frequency of treatment sessions beyond the initial application and duration of the needles)" .

tara-kb:S-112625-756 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-756" ;
    dc:title "The effects of acupuncture after thyroid surgery: A randomized, controlled trial" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol """Hegu-LI4: Located between the first and second metacarpal bones on the dorsal hand region.
Neiguan-Pc6: Located on the anterior side of the forearm, about 5 cm proximal to the palmar wrist crease, between the tendons of the muscle palmaris longus and the muscle flexor carpi radialis.
Chosen for their antinociceptive effects on the cervical region after thyroid surgery.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Randomized Controlled Trial (RCT)" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails "The control group underwent standard postoperative analgesic treatment with acetaminophen. This group was compared to an acupuncture group that received either electroacupuncture (EA) or traditional acupuncture (TA) in addition to standard postoperative analgesic treatment." ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "Italy" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified (the paper mentions registration at ClinicalTrials.gov but does not provide a direct link to a dataset)" ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupoints "LI 4, PC 6" ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupointsMapped TARA:0859695,
        TARA:4311153 ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupointsMappedCurie "{\"TARA:0859695\": \"LI 4\", \"TARA:4311153\": \"PC 6\"}"^^rdf:JSON ;
    tara-kb:hasMappedWesternCondition HP:0012531 ;
    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails "5 mm" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-756 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-756 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails """Total participants: 121
Control group: 62
Acupuncture group: 59
Electroacupuncture (EA): 27
Traditional Acupuncture (TA): 32""" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails "Not applicable (no sham acupuncture group was used in the study)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails """Traditional Acupuncture (TA): Needles were stimulated manually at the insertion until feeling De-Qi sensation (a sensation of numbness and distension) and every 10 minutes.
Electroacupuncture (EA): Needles were stimulated using a dedicated electric stimulator with continued waves at the intensity of 6-8 Volts and a frequency of 25 Hz.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper does not provide a specific TCM disease or condition)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Postoperative pain after thyroid surgery for benign thyroid diseases (euthyroidism or hyperthyroidism)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "Yes, acupuncture was found to be effective compared to the control group, particularly electroacupuncture (EA), which showed significant reductions in acetaminophen consumption and remifentanil use. Traditional acupuncture (TA) did not show significant effects compared to the control group." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measures: Intraoperative pain (measured by remifentanil consumption), postoperative pain (measured by Numeric Rating Scale (NRS), McGill Pain Questionnaire, and acetaminophen consumption)
Secondary outcome measures: Not specified""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """Duration of each acupuncture session: 30 minutes
Duration needles were left in place: Not specified""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "Acupuncture was performed twice: approximately 30 minutes before surgery and in the morning of postoperative day 1. Each session lasted 30 minutes." .

