    tara-kb:hasMappedWesternCondition MONDO:0006862 ;
    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-758 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-758 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails """Total sample size: 22 (15 men, 7 women)
EA group: 16 subjects
TNS group: Not specified""" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails "Not applicable (no sham acupuncture group was used in the study)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails """Electroacupuncture (EA): Electrical stimulation through non insulated acupuncture needles at L14 and P6 points, with a single square wave pulse of 0.5 msec duration at 2 Hz, increasing intensity to induce muscular contractions and a painful but tolerable sensation.
Transcutaneous Nerve Stimulation (TNS): Electrical stimulation through surface electrodes on segmental and heterosegmental nerves, with a single square wave pulse of 0.2 msec duration at 100 Hz, increasing intensity to elicit pleasant tactile sensations.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper does not explicitly mention Traditional Chinese Medicine terminology or conditions)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Myofascial pain syndrome (MPS)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "Yes, electroacupuncture was effective in inducing a significant depression in the R2 response of the blink reflex, with a maximum effect of70% after 20 minutes (P < 0.001). The effect was partially reversible by naloxone, indicating involvement of endogenous opioid systems." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measure: Pain reduction as measured by visual analog scale (VAS)
Secondary outcome measures: Pain pressure threshold, range of motion, self-reported disability, number of tender MTrPs""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """Duration of each acupuncture session: 55 to 60 minutes
Duration needles were left in place: Not specified beyond 30 minutes for recovery cycle study""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "Not specified (the paper does not provide information on the frequency of treatment administration)" .

