    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings """The primary outcome measure showed statistically significant differences in favor of PRP at the second primary outcome time point (p=0.01) and at the secondary outcome time point (p=0.01).
Overall, PRP did not provide significantly greater clinical benefit compared to placebo or dry needling at a six-month follow-up.
There was a marginal clinical advantage in patients with rotator cuff tendinopathy.
Effectiveness: No (overall), Yes (for rotator cuff tendinopathy)""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measure: Pain intensity at two or three and six months after intervention.
Secondary outcome measure: Functional disability at three months after treatment.""" ;
