    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails """Total sample size: 4,578 randomized patients
Completed studies: 4,402 participants
APS groups: 2,097 participants
Control groups: 2,305 participants
Average sample size per study: 73 (range, 18-150)""" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails "Sham procedures involved using needles placed away from meridians (non-acupoint locations), sham acupressure and sham TAES, dummy studs, and sham needle embedding at nonacupoints. These procedures were designed to mimic the intervention without providing therapeutic effects, often using non-penetrating or non-effective methods." ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails """Manual acupuncture: Needles were used with or without heat, pressure, or laser light.
Electroacupuncture (EA): Electrical stimulation was used, with high-frequency EA being more effective than low-frequency EA.
Seed embedding: Magnetic beads or seeds were embedded in skin-colored adhesive tape.
Acupressure: Pressure was applied to specific points.
Plaster therapy: Capsicum plaster was used as an alternative to acupuncture.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper focuses on postoperative pain control, which is a symptom rather than a specific TCM disease or condition)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Postoperative pain" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "Maybe/Inconclusive (the paper indicates some effectiveness in improving VAS scores and reducing morphine consumption, but concludes that there is insufficient evidence to confirm APS as an effective postoperative pain therapy overall)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome: Pain intensity measured by VAS, NRS, or verbal reporting
Secondary outcomes: Analgesic consumption, APS-related adverse events""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration "Not specified in the main text; details likely available in the tables at the end of the paper." ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "Not specified (the paper does not provide explicit information on the frequency of treatment)" .

