    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails "Total sample size: 186; Not specified (exact numbers within each group)" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails """Sham procedure: Attaching patch electrodes to selected acupuncture points.
Needle type: None (patch electrodes used).
Quantity and size: Not specified.
Location: Same as true EA points.
Penetrating or non-penetrating: Non-penetrating.
Other details: Connected to sound-producing dummy mode; same duration and frequency as true EA.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails "Electrical stimulation (electroacupuncture) using biphasic pulses at a frequency of 2 Hz for 20 minutes. No manual manipulation or thermal stimulation was used. The intention was not to elicit De Qi sensation." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper does not explicitly translate the condition into Traditional Chinese Medicine terminology)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Osteoarthritis (OA) of the knee" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "Yes, electroacupuncture was found to be effective compared to the control/sham group based on primary outcomes such as VAS and Lequesne's functional index. The paper states that EA was significantly more effective than placebo and diclofenac in some circumstances, with p-values indicating statistical significance for these outcomes." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcomes: Reduction in symptoms as measured by VAS
Reduction in symptoms as measured by WOMAC
Reduction in symptoms as measured by Lequesne's functional index
Number of paracetamol tablets taken per week
Orthopedist's and patient's overall opinion of change
Secondary outcomes: 50 feet-walk time""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """Duration of each acupuncture session: 20 minutes
Duration needles were left in place: Not specified (inferred to be at least 20 minutes)""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "The frequency of treatment was three times a week (Monday, Wednesday, and Friday) for four weeks, with each session lasting 20 minutes. A total of 12 treatments were administered over the four-week period." .

