tara-kb:S-112625-861 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-861" ;
    dc:title "Acupuncture for shoulder pain (Review)" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol """Traditional or classic acupuncture based on traditional Chinese medicine, involving points where sensations of soreness, numbness, and heaviness are experienced.
Extrapoint of Zhongping.
7 points in the affected shoulder and homolateral upper limb, and 5 additional points (not specified).
Standard acupuncture points: SI9, SI3, GV 14, TE 15, TE 14, LI 15, LI11.
Deep acupuncture: needles placed 25mm into skin.
Shallow acupuncture: needles placed 4mm into skin.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Systematic Review" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails """Placebo: Used in two trials to compare with acupuncture for rotator cuff disease.
Steroid injection: Compared to acupuncture in one trial for rotator cuff disease.
Ultrasound: Compared to acupuncture in one trial for rotator cuff disease.
Exercise alone: Compared to acupuncture plus exercise in one trial for adhesive capsulitis.
Tragar: Compared to acupuncture in one trial for general shoulder pain due to wheelchair use.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasMappedWesternCondition HP:0030834,
        MONDO:0005178 ;
    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails """6.5 cm (Group 1: Acupuncture plus exercise)
1-3 cm (Group 1: Acupuncture and mobilisation)
25mm (Group 1: Deep acupuncture)
4mm (Group 2: Shallow acupuncture)
1-3 cm (Group 1: Acupuncture consisting of two 20-30 minute sessions)""" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-861 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-861 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails "Total participants: over 500; Individual trial sample sizes range from 18 to 150, with a median of 44. Specific group sizes within trials are not specified." ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails """Berry 1980: Sham procedure involved placebo with no needle penetration of the skin.
Kleinhenz 1999: No specific sham details provided.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails """Manual stimulation: Strong stimulation from wide amplitude needle rotation simultaneously with lifting and thrusting movements to evoke sensations of heaviness, soreness, numbness.
Rotatory left and right movements for 30 seconds every 5 minutes.
Moxibustion (thermal stimulation) was used in one trial.
Sensations sought: Soreness, numbness, heaviness.
No mention of electrical stimulation (electroacupuncture) or laser stimulation.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Shoulder pain, specifically adhesive capsulitis (frozen shoulder), rotator cuff disease, and osteoarthritis." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "Maybe/Inconclusive (the paper indicates some short-term benefits but concludes that overall evidence is insufficient to support or refute acupuncture's effectiveness for shoulder pain)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure "Primary outcomes: Pain, shoulder function (measured by the Constant Murley Score), range of motion. Secondary outcomes: Time to maximum pain relief, shoulder discomfort, success or failure of treatment, adverse events." ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """Duration of each acupuncture session: Typically 20-30 minutes
Duration needles were left in place: For the duration of the session (20-30 minutes)""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "Treatments were typically conducted two or three times a week for three to six weeks, with each session lasting twenty to thirty minutes. Exceptions include once a week for three weeks in the Moore trial and every second day for five treatments in the Yuan trial." .

