tara-kb:S-112625-869 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-869" ;
    dc:title "Acupuncture for Chronic Shoulder Pain in Persons With Spinal Cord Injury: A Small-Scale Clinical Trial" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol """Local points:
C1: Anterior aspect of deltoid, 1 cun lateral to M-UE-48 (Jianneiling), midway between LI 15 (Jianyu) and top of anterior axillary fold.
C2: Middle of deltoid muscle, 1 cun proximal to N-UE-14 (Naoshang), center of triangle formed by LI 15 (Jianyu), SJ 14 (Jianliao), and N-UE-14 (Naoshang).
C3: Supraspinatus muscle, anterior and inferior to acromion, cun proximal to LI 15 (Jianyu) and 1 cun inferior to LI 16 (Jugu).
C4: Posterior and inferior to acromion, 1 cun inferior to SJ 14 (Jianliao) and 1 cun medial to SJ meridian.
C5: Scapula, line midway between SI 9 (Jianshen) and SI 10 (Naoshu).
C6: Line midway between GB 21 (Jianjing) and acromion.
Distal points:
C7: Dorsal aspect of forearm, 1 cun lateral to LI 10 (Shousanli), midway between LI 10 and radius.
C8: Dorsal aspect of hand, midway between LI 4 (Hegu) and LI 5 (Yangxi), 1 cun lateral to LI meridian, above first metacarpal bone.
Ashi points: Local points of tenderness or sensitivity in the shoulder region.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Randomized Controlled Trial (RCT)" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails "Invasive sham acupuncture (light needling of nonacupuncture points) was used as the control group. This involved shallow needling with no manipulation at sites located at least 1 cun away from established meridian points and extra points on the shoulder." ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupoints "LI 15, SJ 14, LI 16, SI 9, SI 10, GB 21, LI 10, LI 4, LI 5" ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupointsMapped TARA:0683404,
        TARA:0742938,
        TARA:0859695,
        TARA:1252750,
        TARA:2187225,
        TARA:5389461,
        TARA:5677319,
        TARA:5894876,
        TARA:7829474 ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupointsMappedCurie "{\"TARA:7829474\": \"LI 15\", \"TARA:5894876\": \"TE 14\", \"TARA:1252750\": \"LI 16\", \"TARA:2187225\": \"SI 9\", \"TARA:0683404\": \"SI 10\", \"TARA:5389461\": \"GB 21\", \"TARA:0742938\": \"LI 10\", \"TARA:0859695\": \"LI 4\", \"TARA:5677319\": \"LI 5\"}"^^rdf:JSON ;
    tara-kb:hasMappedWesternCondition MONDO:0043797 ;
    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails """Acupuncture: 1 to 3cm
Sham Acupuncture: Most superficial depth (not specified in mm or cm)""" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-869 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-869 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails "Total: 17; Acupuncture: 8; Sham Acupuncture: 9" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails """Type: Invasive sham acupuncture using real needles
Quantity: 8 points per painful shoulder (6 local, 2 distal)
Location: Non-acupuncture points, at least 1 cun away from established meridian points
Penetration: Shallow needling to superficial depth
Manipulation: No manipulation; needles were tapped in and left upright
DeQi: Avoided""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails "Manual stimulation to acquire DeQi (a sensation of heaviness, soreness, or numbness) for the acupuncture group; no actual stimulation for the sham acupuncture group, with pretended stimulation to maintain the blind." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Chronic musculoskeletal shoulder pain in subjects with spinal cord injury (SCI)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "Maybe/Inconclusive (The study found significant pain reduction in both groups, but no significant difference between acupuncture and sham acupuncture due to small sample size and lack of statistical significance.)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measure: Changes in shoulder pain intensity measured using the Wheelchair User's Shoulder Pain Index (WUSPI)
Secondary outcome measure: NRS end-point average pain intensity score""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """Duration of needles in place: 20 minutes
Duration of each acupuncture session: Not specified""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "Approximately twice weekly for 5 weeks (range, 5‚Äì8 weeks)" .

tara-kb:S-112625-87 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-87" ;
    dc:title "Severe knee osteoarthritis: a randomized controlled trial of acupuncture, physiotherapy (supervised exercise) and standard management for patients awaiting knee replacement" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol "Not specified (the paper refers to Figure 1 for specific points but does not provide the content of Figure 1, and additional points were used at the physiotherapist's discretion without specification)" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Randomized Controlled Trial (RCT)" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails "The control group received an exercise and advice leaflet and was referred to as the \"home exercise group.\" They followed a standardized advice and exercise regimen at home without receiving acupuncture or physiotherapy interventions." ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "United Kingdom" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasListedAcupoints "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasMappedWesternCondition HP:0005086 ;
    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails "1 inch (2.54 cm)" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-87 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-87 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails """Total sample size: 181
Acupuncture group: 60
Physiotherapy group: 60
Standard management group: 61""" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails "Not applicable (no sham acupuncture group was used in the study)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails "The needles were stimulated by achieving de qi where possible. No other forms of stimulation such as manual manipulation, electrical stimulation, thermal stimulation, or laser stimulation were mentioned." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper does not provide a TCM diagnosis or condition)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Severe knee osteoarthritis" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "Yes, acupuncture was effective compared to the control group in reducing OKS at 7 weeks (p=0.0497), but the effect was not sustained at 12 weeks." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measure: Oxford Knee Score (OKS) questionnaire
Secondary outcome measures: 50 m timed walk, duration of hospital stay following knee arthroplasty, Western Ontario MacMaster (WOMAC) Score, 10-cm pain visual analogue scale (VAS), Hospital Anxiety and Depression score (HAD)""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """Duration of treatment: Once a week for 6 weeks
Duration of each session: Needles left in place for 20 minutes""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency "The acupuncture treatments were administered once a week for 6 weeks, with each session lasting 20 minutes." .

