Participants within groups: Specified in Table 1 on page 6""" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails """Sham procedure involved using a superficial needle at the acupuncture point.
Wang's study used the anti-Ashi point for sham acupuncture.
Sham acupuncture was performed on unaffected points or using sham acupuncture on the same points as the experimental group.
Sham procedure was likely non-penetrating or involved superficial needling.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails """Manual Acupuncture: Mechanical stimulation to input Qi or energy flow, seeking De Qi sensation.
Laser Acupuncture: Low-level laser therapy to induce biological stimulation.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Lateral Epicondylalgia (Heat Bi or Cold Bi)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Lateral epicondylalgia (Tennis elbow)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "Yes, manual acupuncture was found to be effective compared to the control/sham group for short-term pain relief, but its long-term effect is unremarkable. Laser acupuncture was not found to be effective." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measure: Pain relief (measured using the visual analog scale (VAS))
Secondary outcome measures:
Muscle strength (assessed through forearm movements against resistance)
Upper limb function (evaluated using the DASH questionnaire)
Patient satisfaction (assessed using a self-satisfaction questionnaire)""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """Manual Acupuncture: 25-30 minutes per session
Laser Acupuncture: 30 seconds to 2 minutes per session""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency """Manual acupuncture: 2 times weekly (Fink et al., 2002a, 2002b), 2‚Äì3 times weekly (Haker and Lundeberg, 1990a, 1990b)
Laser acupuncture: 3‚Äì4 times weekly (Haker and Lundeberg, 1991), 2 times weekly (Lundeberg et al., 1987)
Number of sessions: 1-5 sessions (Wang, Gu et al., Molsberger et al., Lundeberg et al.), 10 sessions (other studies)
Duration of sessions: Manual acupuncture 25-30 minutes, Laser acupuncture 30 seconds to 2 minutes""" .

