tara-kb:S-112625-932 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-932" ;
    dc:title "Acupuncture in the Treatment of Pain in Temporomandibular Disorders: A Systematic Review and Meta-analysis of Randomized Controlled Trials" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol """Proximal points: ST 6, ST 7, GB 20, EX 2
Distal points: LI 4, SI 2, SI 3, ST 36
Most frequently used acupoint: LI 4
Insertion depth: varied from 6 to 30 mm""" ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Systematic Review and Meta-analysis of Randomized Controlled Trials (RCTs)" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails """Occlusal splint
Sham acupuncture
Sham laser acupuncture
No treatment""" ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "Not specified (the paper does not explicitly mention the country or countries where the study was conducted)" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasMappedWesternCondition MONDO:0005473 ;
    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails "6 to 30 mm" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-932 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-932 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails """Total sample size for meta-analysis: n=96
Sample sizes within groups:
Goddard et al: SG=10, CG=8
Schmid-Schwap et al: SG=11, CG=12
Smith et al: SG=15, CG=12
Shen et al: SG=16, CG=12""" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails """Sham acupuncture was performed by needling outside of acupuncture points.
Non-penetrative sham acupuncture was used in some studies.
Sham laser acupuncture was applied randomly on several acupuncture points.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails "Manual stimulation was used in all studies except for three, where electroacupuncture was combined with manual acupuncture. The duration of each session varied between 15 to 30 minutes. Insertion depth varied from 6 to 30 mm. No specific sensations were mentioned as being sought during stimulation." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified (the paper does not explicitly mention TCM terminology for the condition)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Temporomandibular Disorders (TMDs)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "Yes, acupuncture is effective compared to the control/sham group(s) based on the primary outcome(s). The meta-analysis found that acupuncture is more effective than placebo in reducing pain intensity in TMD patients, with a statistically significant standardized mean difference of 0.83 (95% CI, 0.41-1.25; P=0.00012)." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure "Primary outcome measure: Intensity of pain according to the visual analog scale (VAS). Secondary outcome measures: Improvements in masticatory function and maximum interincisal opening." ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """Duration of each session: varied between 15 to 30 minutes, with 30 minutes being the most common.
Duration needles were left in place: Not specified.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency """Duration of each session: varied between 15 to 30 minutes, with 30 minutes being the most common.
Number of sessions: Four studies had only one session; the rest had a mean of 6 to 8 sessions.
Frequency of treatment: Not specified (e.g., daily, weekly).""" .

