tara-kb:S-112625-941 a TARA:6701896,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:identifier "tara-kb:S-112625-941" ;
    dc:title "Acupuncture for treating temporomandibular joint disorders: A systematic review and meta-analysis of randomized, sham-controlled trials" ;
    tara-kb:hasAcupunctureProtocol "LI 4 (used 34 times), SI 3 (used 19 times), ST 6 (used 13 times), SI 18 (used 12 times), SI 2 (used 7 times), ST 7 (used 6 times). Locations: Face (mandible), Hand. Criteria for selection: Not specified." ;
    tara-kb:hasClinicalTrialType "Systematic Review of Randomized Controlled Trials (RCTs)" ;
    tara-kb:hasControlGroupsDetails "Sham acupuncture (penetrating, non-penetrating, or sham laser)" ;
    tara-kb:hasCountryOfStudy "United States, Austria, United Kingdom, Germany" ;
    tara-kb:hasDatasetLink "Not specified (the paper mentions supplementary data but does not explicitly provide a dataset link)" ;
    tara-kb:hasMappedWesternCondition MONDO:0005473 ;
    tara-kb:hasNeedlingDetails "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasOCSIAppraisalID tara-kb:O-112625-941 ;
    tara-kb:hasReportingPublicationID tara-kb:A-112625-941 ;
    tara-kb:hasSampleSizeDetails """Total sample size: 141
Mean sample size per study: 20
Gender distribution: female:male = 19.1:1
Mean age: 37.3 years
Specific sample sizes for analyses:
Pooled meta-analysis: n = 107
Muscular type TMD: n = 84
Combined type TMD: n = 23
Non-penetrating sham control methods: n = 89
Penetrating sham method: n = 18""" ;
    tara-kb:hasShamAcupunctureDetails """Sham procedures included both penetrating and non-penetrating needles.
Penetrating sham involved using a needle on non-acupoints.
Non-penetrating sham involved using a needle on acupoints or non-acupoints without penetration.
The sham methods were designed to mimic the real acupuncture experience for the control group.
Specific details about each study's sham procedure are provided in the tables at the end of the paper.""" ;
    tara-kb:hasStimulationTypeDetails "Not specified (the paper does not provide specific details on how needles were stimulated or if any sensation was sought)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedTCMCondition "Not specified" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudiedWesternCondition "Temporomandibular joint disorder (TMD)" ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyFindings "Yes, acupuncture was found to be more effective than sham acupuncture in alleviating pain and masseter muscle tenderness in TMD, with significant improvements in pain intensity (p = 0.0004). However, the evidence is considered weak due to methodological limitations." ;
    tara-kb:hasStudyOutcomesMeasure """Primary outcome measures: Pain intensity or pain relief in TMJ measured by visual analogue scale (VAS), verbal scale, or algometer.
Secondary outcome measures: Maximum inter-incisal mouth opening (MO), range of motion (ROM) of TMJ, response rate (responder vs. non-responder), facial pain using NRS, muscle tenderness (4 point scale).""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentDuration """Duration of treatment period: Ranged from 1 treatment to 3 weeks (mean: 1.4 weeks)
Number of sessions: Four studies used single acupuncture treatments; three studies used 6-12 sessions
Duration of each session: Not specified
Duration needles were left in place: Not specified""" ;
    tara-kb:hasTreatmentFrequency """Duration of total treatment: 1 treatment to 3 weeks (mean: 1.4 weeks)
Frequency: Four studies used single treatments; three studies used 6-12 treatments
Specific examples:
Katsoulis (2010): 2 treatments/week for 3 weeks
Smith (2007): 6 treatments over 3 weeks
Schmid-Schwab (2006): 6 treatments over 3 weeks""" .

