TARA:1132428 a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Acupuncture Point",
        "Acupuncture Point"@en ;
    IAO:0000115 "A fiat object part of the human body, delimited by the practice of needling rather than by any natural physical discontinuity, consisting of a surface locus where a needle is inserted together with the shallow underlying tissue it penetrates, without commitment to the exact depth or angle of penetration."@en ;
    IAO:0000116 "Acupoint is modeled as a subclass of 'fiat object part' (BFO:0000024): An acupoint is a fiat-demarcated volume carved out of whatever pre-existing material entities (skin, subcutaneous tissue, fascia, muscle) occupy that location, delimited by the practice of needling. Its identity as, e.g., \"LU 1\", as distinct from the surrounding tissue, comes entirely from TCM/acupuncture convention, not from any physical discontinuity or histological boundary."@en ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Fang Li, Tian He, Qian Xu, Li-Ting Lin, Hui Li, Yan Liu, Guang-Xia Shi, Cun-Zhi Liu, What is the Acupoint? A preliminary review of Acupoints, Pain Medicine, Volume 16, Issue 10, October 2015, Pages 1905–1915, https://doi.org/10.1111/pme.12761" ;
    dcterms:creator "Fahim Imam - https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4752-543X" ;
    dcterms:description "According to traditional Chinese medicine (TCM) theory, acupoints are specifically chosen sites of acupuncture manipulation, and also the basis for studying the mechanism of acupuncture. Stimulating different acupoints on the body surface could provide various therapeutic benefits."@en ;
    tara-ap:hasSynonym "Acupoint" .

